 goes into that call's result, so expect no follow-up turn when it ends. A later resume that nothing waits for starts a new run without the field.",HA="task_id of the subagent (local_agent) task whose agent launched this subagent, shell, monitor or workflow task. Absent when the launcher is the main thread, has no task_id of its own (a teammate, an agent inside a workflow), or is no longer tracked. A resumed subagent keeps its original parent. The parent may be a foreground or already-ended task, so treat an unknown id as no parent.",Yt="Id of one run of the task: equal on every task_* event, background_tasks_changed entry and saved notification (its origin.runId) of that run. A resumed task keeps its task_id and gets a new one. The run ids of one task sort, by plain string comparison, in the order the runs opened; a frame that names a run sorting before the one a reader shows is late. Nothing else about the format is promised. Absent for a task this process did not register, and from CLIs that predate it.",cW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("task_notification"),task_id:o(),run_id:o().optional().describe(Yt),tool_use_id:o().optional(),status:B(["completed","failed","stopped"]),reason:B(["worker_restart"]).optional().describe("Machine-readable cause, set only when the task did not end through an ordinary completion, failure, or stop. 'worker_restart': the worker process restarted and the resumed process found the task orphaned (always with status 'stopped')."),output_file:o(),summary:o(),usage:u({total_tokens:E().int(),tool_uses:E().int(),duration_ms:E().int()}).optional(),resource_links:C(CA()).optional().describe("CLI-owned: for a backgrounded MCP task (task_type mcp_task) that completed, the `resource_link` content blocks of its final result \u2014 the files it returned by reference \u2014 collected from the raw result before the CLI renders it as the text the model reads. A backgrounded task's tool_result is the placeholder text and its real result arrives as this notification, so this is where a host learns which files that tool call produced; join to the originating call via tool_use_id. Same fields and caps as tool_use_result.resourceLinks (at most 50 links, 64 KiB serialized), absent when the result had none or the task is any other type. Never populated from the server's _meta."),handback:B(lKr).optional().describe("@internal Present on a 'completed' notification of a subagent that reports through the SubagentHandback tool: how its report reached its caller, with the values and meaning of `handback` on AgentToolCompletedOutput. Left out when the subagent failed, was stopped, is waiting on agents it started, or is waiting on its own background work with nothing delivered yet; for such a subagent, `summary` is then not a report. When present, `summary` holds harness notes written for the model. Any notification can be followed by a later one for the same task_id when the agent is resumed or finishes its wait; the later one replaces what the earlier one showed."),handback_report:dKr().optional().describe("@internal The report a 'send' or 'flagged' hand-back delivered, the same shape as `handbackReport` on AgentToolCompletedOutput: when present, render it instead of `summary`, with its `warning`, if it has one, above its `text`. Absent for 'withheld'; if it is absent for another value too, show `summary`."),skip_transcript:O().optional(),ambient:O().optional().describe(Eo),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),hW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("task_started"),task_id:o(),run_id:o().optional().describe(Yt),tool_use_id:o().optional(),description:o(),subagent_type:o().optional().describe("Subagent type for Task tool subagents."),is_backgrounded:O().optional().describe("Whether the task was registered in the background (true) or in the foreground with the spawning tool call blocking on it (false). A resumed subagent is always registered in the background. A later move to the background arrives as task_updated patch.is_backgrounded. Set for local_agent and local_bash tasks."),awaited:O().optional().describe(KA),spawn_depth:E().int().optional().describe("Nesting depth of a spawned subagent (local_agent) task: 1 for a top-level spawn, N+1 when spawned from inside a depth-N agent. Not set on other tasks."),parent_task_id:o().optional().describe(HA),task_type:o().optional(),workflow_name:o().optional().describe("meta.name from the workflow script (e.g. 'spec'). Only set when task_type is 'local_workflow'."),prompt:o().optional(),skip_transcript:O().optional().describe("Ambient/housekeeping task. Consumers should hide this from the inline transcript; it may still appear in a tasks panel."),ambient:O().optional().describe(Eo),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),uW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("task_updated"),task_id:o(),run_id:o().optional().describe(Yt),patch:u({status:B(["pending","running","completed","failed","killed","paused"]).optional(),description:o().optional(),end_time:E().int().optional(),total_paused_ms:E().int().optional(),error:o().optional(),is_backgrounded:O().optional()}).describe("Wire-safe subset of TaskState fields that changed. Excludes abortController, messages, result. Clients merge into their local task map."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),pW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("background_tasks_changed"),tasks:C(u({task_id:o(),run_id:o().optional().describe(Yt),task_type:o(),subagent_type:o().optional().describe(`Agent type of a local_agent task, such as "general-purpose" or a custom agent's name; "main-session" for a backgrounded main session. Not set on other tasks.`),description:o(),parent_task_id:o().optional().describe(HA),ambient:O().optional().describe(Eo),awaited:O().optional().describe(KA)})).describe("Every live background task after the change. REPLACE semantics: swap your set for this payload."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("The full set of live background tasks, emitted whenever membership changes (start, completion, kill, a foreground agent being backgrounded) or an entry's `ambient` flag or `parent_task_id` changes. A level signal, unlike the task_started/task_notification edge bookends: consumers that only need 'is background work running' should replace their set with each payload rather than pairing edges, so a missed bookend cannot wedge a stale running indicator. Ordering relative to the bookends for the same transition is unspecified (in practice a task that starts in the background joins the level before its task_started, and a finishing task leaves it after its task_updated and task_notification), so do not correlate it with the edge stream. The level is per-process: nothing is emitted at startup, so consumers must reset to the empty set whenever the session's CLI process (re)starts and let the next membership change repopulate it. A host that re-initializes an already-running process (a repeated `initialize` control request, e.g. after reconnecting) is sent a snapshot of the current set right behind the success response to that request, even when it is empty, so it need not wait for a change; CLIs that predate this send nothing there.")),mW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("feedback_draft_queued"),draft_id:o().describe("UUID of the on-disk draft file under ~/.claude/feedback/drafts/ \u2014 the identity for discard and submit."),draft_type:B(["bug","idea","missing_capability"]),title:o().describe("Sanitized one-line summary from the SendFeedback tool call."),details_preview:o().describe("First ~200 chars of the sanitized details, for the host UI's three-line card preview. The full draft stays on disk and is read when the user opens the review form."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted by the SendFeedback tool after it writes a local draft to ~/.claude/feedback/drafts/, so a connected host UI can surface a review card without polling the filesystem. Carries only the card's display fields; nothing is sent anywhere without the user's explicit action on the review form.")),_W=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("session_state_changed"),state:B(["idle","running","requires_action"]),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Mirrors notifySessionStateChanged. 'idle' fires after heldBackResult flushes and the bg-agent do-while exits \u2014 authoritative turn-over signal.")),gW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("session_title_changed"),title:o().describe("The session's current name. Control, bidirectional and zero-width characters are replaced with single spaces, the ends are trimmed, and the name is at most 200 Unicode code points. When another session already has the name, a suffix can make it unique."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal The session's name, for a host that shows it, such as an IDE editor tab. A headless (print mode) session sends it at startup when the session already has a name, and again after each change to the name (not an AI-generated title), including a rename the host itself sent with `rename_session`. A name that another process writes to the transcript arrives when this process next reads the end of its transcript: after about 32 KB of its own transcript writes, or at a compaction. A cleared name is not sent. The same name can arrive more than once, for example when it is set again, so a host should treat a repeated name as no change. The frame can arrive before the system/init message that first names its session_id, for example at startup or after /clear. A host that learns the session id from system/init can hold the name until then.")),fW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("worker_shutting_down"),reason:o().describe("Short snake_case reason set by the host CLI (not user input), e.g. 'host_exit', 'remote_control_disabled'."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Emitted by the bridge on opt-in graceful worker teardown (only when the teardown caller supplied a reason), before the heartbeat stops, so remote clients can show why the worker went away instead of waiting for heartbeat timeout. Absence is NOT a dead-host signal: handoffs (/update, /teleport, respawn), auto-disable, mode transitions, and internal fatal-error paths emit nothing by design. A dead host (battery, OOM, kill -9) never reaches teardown and never sends this either. NOTE: this event lands in the durable per-session event stream \u2014 a session that is later resumed may carry historical instances mid-stream. Clients MUST treat it as a live-tail signal only (honored when no further activity follows), not a one-shot session-lifetime fact.")),bW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("turn_handoff_available"),v:T(1),tools:C(o()),worker_epoch:E(),uuid:p(),session_id:o(),staged_files:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Present, and true, when this worker starts a handed-off turn's calls only after every stage_file control request it received earlier has finished or failed, or a wait limit has passed; a worker that omits it may start them while files are still downloading. Exception: a request with filestore_path set (a synced file) is not waited for, so a call that names that file may start before it is synced."),no_query_first:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal True when user messages with shouldQuery false that reach this worker before a turn_handoff request are appended, and acknowledged, before the handed-off turn produces any output. Exception: a request that continues a turn whose leading lines an earlier request appended; that turn goes first. A worker that omits it may run the turn first."),shared_message_id:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal True when this worker accepts a turn_handoff request in which an assistant message repeats the message id of the assistant message before it, as the messages member of that request describes. A worker that omits it refuses such a request (invalid_handoff), so a client gives each message an id of its own there."),relay_marker:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Present, and true, only when this worker uses the relay_marker member of a turn_handoff request; a client sends that member to no other worker."),home_files:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Present, and true, only on a worker that takes a stage_file control request whose mount_path is /uploads/.home/<path> as a file for /home/claude/<path>: it writes the file there, answers ok with a noop (already_present, path_occupied, refused_name, home_files_off, unchecked_announcement, resent_announcement) and writes nothing, or answers an error. Any other worker treats that path as an ordinary upload."),file_names:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Present, and true, only when this worker uses the file_names member of a turn_handoff request; a client sends that member to no other worker."),carried_writes:T(!0).optional().describe(`@internal Present, and true, only on a worker that writes the files of the Write calls a turn_handoff request brings already answered, where their paths are under /home/claude. Before it appends a turn that has calls to run, it writes each such call's content to its path, replacing nothing. If it cannot write them all, it appends and runs nothing: the request is refused with an error that starts "invalid_handoff: carried_writes_", or it is accepted and the turn ends in a result with num_turns 0. Any other worker writes no file for these calls.`),hydrates_carried_lines:T(!0).optional().describe(`@internal Present, and true, only on a worker that rebuilt the conversation from the session's stored transcript before it read any request, and would not have started without it. A client may send this worker again a turn_handoff request that an earlier worker accepted, if the request names no calls (tool_use_ids empty), is not stopped, and its messages hold no tool call. A request whose turn had not ended when an earlier worker with this key stopped is left unacknowledged, so the session service sends it to this worker by itself; a second request for the same turn is answered "duplicate" while the first waits or runs. The worker appends only the messages of the turn that its conversation lacks and, once the store has confirmed every message of the turn, sends the turn's last message back as an assistant event (the echo); a turn it already holds whole is echoed on the same condition. The request can still be accepted, or refused ("invalid_handoff:"), with no echo, and the turn may then not be stored. That happens, for example, when the conversation has moved on past the part of the turn it holds, when a rewind has removed the turn, when a compaction has summarised part of it (or all of it, if the compaction was this worker's own), when a Stop clears the queue first, or when the store does not confirm the messages. A request sent again waits again for messages not yet confirmed. When a delivery finds that the store refused a message of the turn, it sends no echo and removes the turn from the conversation, and the same request sent again to that worker gets no echo. If another row has followed the turn, the turn stays, also with no echo. Exception: after 512 later refused rows the worker forgets a refusal and reads the message as confirmed. Confirmed means that the store answered the upload with success. A turn that an earlier worker's compaction summarised whole is appended a second time. The key promises nothing for a turn that holds a tool call, or whose first message is stored with no answer but with another row behind it (a hook's output, say): the worker may already have continued such a turn itself when it started. A worker without the key may have started with none of the turn's messages.`)}).describe("@internal Emitted once by a cloud worker that accepts the turn_handoff control request, after it registers, carrying what it wrote to external_metadata.turn_handoff: the version, the tools whose calls it would accept, the worker life (worker_epoch) announcing it, whether handed-off calls wait for files still being staged (staged_files), whether user messages that make no model call are appended before a handed-off turn (no_query_first), whether two assistant messages of a turn may share a message id (shared_message_id), and relay_marker, home_files, file_names, carried_writes and hydrates_carried_lines when set. Lets a session client learn the capability from the event stream instead of reading worker state. Durable in the stream: a reader keeps the entry with the newest worker_epoch it has seen and ignores older ones; a worker life that does not accept turn_handoff emits nothing (its registration writes turn_handoff: null).")),EW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_invalidate"),event:T("ui.render").describe("@internal The render event whose version moved; the engine versions `ui.render` as one event, so no component or instance is named."),prompts_screened:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Present while a mod may change what is recorded of a typed line (a hook on `prompt.submit`, `command.run`, `session.append` or, for a line sent to the session, `session.receive`; mods not yet known; or an organization policy that lists a plugin or cannot be read): what the `prompts_screened_v1` capability says on `system/init`, said as soon as a plugin load or reload makes it so. Absent says nothing: the next `system/init` does."),instances:C(u({surface:o().describe("@internal The surface that drew the instance."),component:o().describe("@internal The component of the instance."),instance_id:o().describe("@internal The engine's requestId of the instance, as the ui_render that drew it named it.")})).optional().describe("@internal Present when a plugin wrote state (`$.state.set`) that these instances read while they were drawn, or a `Client` drawn in them failed and its plugin heard it (`ui.fault`), and nothing else moved: only they are stale, and the surface may re-ask them alone. Absent, every mounted instance may be stale, as before. A surface that ignores the field re-asks everything and stays correct."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe('@internal Fire-and-forget push from the hooks engine when a plugin called `$.ui.invalidate("ui.render")` or a plugin loaded or reloaded: every tree a remote surface drew through ui_render may be stale. The surface re-asks each mounted instance; the engine dedups the evaluations. Bumps from one plugin fold to at most one per 100 ms. With `instances`, a state write or a `Client` that failed made only those stale.')),SW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_log"),plugin:o().describe("@internal The calling plugin's registry name, as the hooks engine knows it (never plugin-settable)."),text:o().describe("@internal The line the plugin gave: be ready for up to 10000 characters of it and then an ellipsis. Plain text: draw it as a text node, never as markup."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fire-and-forget push from a headless session for one `$.ui.log(text)` line: what the REPL appends to its transcript as a dim notice under the plugin's name. Not sent to the model. Lines are not counted across the session: a plugin's lines are the plugin's to write, however long the session runs; ones logged before the stream opened are delivered first, in order.")),TW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_toast"),plugin:o().describe("@internal The calling plugin's registry name, as the hooks engine knows it (never plugin-settable)."),text:o().describe("@internal The line the plugin gave: be ready for up to 10000 characters of it and then an ellipsis. Plain text: draw it as a text node, never as markup."),timeout_ms:E().int().describe("@internal How long the REPL would keep it on the notification bar: the plugin's `timeoutMs`, or the engine's default (4000)."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fire-and-forget push from a headless session for one `$.ui.toast(text)`: what the REPL shows on the notification bar under the prompt for a few seconds, under the plugin's name. Be ready for a plugin's toasts at no fixed rate; nothing counts how many a plugin has shown.")),wW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_status"),plugin:o().describe("@internal The calling plugin's registry name, as the hooks engine knows it (never plugin-settable). One status line per plugin."),text:o().nullable().describe("@internal The plugin's pinned line, replacing its previous one; null removes it. Be ready for up to 10000 characters of it and then an ellipsis. Plain text: draw it as a text node, never as markup."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fire-and-forget push from a headless session for one `$.ui.status(text)`: the line the REPL pins under the prompt beside the engine's own notices until the plugin's next call replaces or clears it.")),yW=/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}$/,pe=f(()=>o().regex(yW).describe("@internal A pane's id as the plugin opened it (`$.ui.open({ id })`): 1 to 64 of letters, digits, `_`, `-`. What a ui_render for `Pane` carries as `instance_id`.")),AW=f(()=>u({id:pe(),title:o().describe("@internal The pane's tab label: the `title` its latest open gave, else its id. Plugin-authored text the engine bounded and freed of control characters: draw it as a text node, never as markup."),plugin:o().describe("@internal The registry name of the plugin that opened it, as the engine knows it; a `$.ui.scroll` or `$.ui.focus` of the pane is that plugin's alone."),close_on_escape:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Escape while the pane holds the keyboard, or at an idle empty composer, closes it as the person's close does (send ui_close); absent, Escape only returns the keyboard."),hold_toasts:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal The surface holds its transient toasts while this pane is the one shown, and shows them once it closes."),rows:E().int().optional().describe("@internal The body rows its content asked for while seated inline; a request the person's own sizing overrides."),columns:E().int().optional().describe("@internal The body columns its content asked for while docked; a request the person's own sizing overrides.")}).describe("@internal One placed pane as the engine holds it (plugins/functionHooks/panes/): which, its tab, its opener, and the manners and size its latest open asked for.")),So=f(()=>u({panes:C(AW()).describe("@internal The placed open panes in open order (an open appends, a close removes); a pane waiting unplaced is not listed. The surface draws one container for them: tabs when several, the shown one visible."),shown_id:pe().nullable().describe("@internal The pane the surface shows: the last opened or focused, never displacing one the person holds; null with none open."),focused_id:pe().nullable().describe("@internal The pane the person gave the keyboard (ui_pane_focus); null while the composer has it."),focus_requested_id:pe().nullable().describe("@internal A standing `$.ui.open({ focus: true })` the surface has yet to judge: honour it (focus the pane, send ui_pane_focus with its id) only while the composer is empty and idle and no dialog is up, else refuse it (ui_pane_focus with null).")}).describe("@internal The session's panes as a remote surface draws them: what ui_panes answers and the ui_panes push carries.")),RW=f(()=>So().extend({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_panes"),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fire-and-forget push from a headless session whenever the pane roster a remote surface draws changes: a plugin opened, retitled or closed a pane, the person showed, focused or closed one, a placing client attached. Deduplicated: an unchanged roster is not pushed. A pane opened before the stream armed produces none; read the roster with ui_panes on mount.")),vW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_scroll"),client_id:o().describe("@internal The client drawing the site, as it attached."),component:B(["Pane","AbovePrompt"]).describe("@internal Which site: a pane's body or the band."),instance_id:o().describe("@internal The pane's id, or `above-prompt` for the band."),offset:E().int().describe("@internal The first content row the window is to show, 0 at the top, clamped to the tree as the surface last reported it."),follow_end:O().optional().describe("@internal True: keep the window on the tree's last rows through the draws to come, until the person or a plugin next moves it."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fire-and-forget push when a plugin's `$.ui.scroll` (through the `ui.scroll` chain) moved the window of a site a remote surface draws: the surface scrolls the site's body there. The person's own scrolls (ui_scroll requests) are never pushed back.")),kW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("ui_focus"),client_id:o().describe("@internal The client drawing the site, as it attached."),component:B(["Pane","AbovePrompt"]).describe("@internal Which site: a pane's body or the band."),instance_id:o().describe("@internal The pane's id, or `above-prompt` for the band."),plugin:o().describe("@internal Whose element takes the ring: its `press.plugin`."),key:o().describe("@internal The element's `props.key`, as ui.press names it."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fire-and-forget push when a plugin's `$.ui.focus` (through the `ui.focus` chain) moved the focus ring of a site a remote surface draws onto one of its Buttons, Inputs or Selects: the surface focuses that element if it still draws it. The person's own moves (ui_focus requests) are never pushed back.")),zW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("commands_changed"),commands:C(Wt()),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Fire-and-forget push of the full slash-command list after a mid-session change (e.g. skills discovered dynamically as the agent works in a subdirectory). Clients should REPLACE their cached command list with this payload; supportedCommands() tracks the latest push, so a re-fetch returns the same fresh list.")),CW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("notification"),key:o(),text:o(),priority:B(["low","medium","high","immediate"]),color:o().optional(),timeout_ms:E().int().optional(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Loop-side text notification. Mirrors the interactive REPL notification queue (key/priority/timeout). JSX notifications are not emitted on this channel.")),OW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("task_progress"),task_id:o(),run_id:o().optional().describe(Yt),tool_use_id:o().optional(),description:o(),subagent_type:o().optional().describe("Subagent type for Task tool subagents."),usage:u({total_tokens:E().int(),tool_uses:E().int(),duration_ms:E().int()}),last_tool_name:o().optional(),summary:o().optional().describe("A one-line status for the task's row. For a local_agent task it is the model-generated progress summary (only when the agentProgressSummaries option is on); for a backgrounded mcp_task it is the MCP server's own bounded status message, emitted once per change without any option. Render it when present regardless of task type."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})),NW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("thinking_tokens"),estimated_tokens:E().int(),estimated_tokens_delta:E().int(),user_message_uuid:o().optional().describe("Client uuid of the user message that triggered this turn (submitMessage options.uuid), stamped on every thinking_tokens frame of a headless (stream-json / Agent SDK) turn so a consumer can attribute thinking progress to the send it answers before any reply frame arrives. On a synthetic/scheduled (meta) turn it names the last user message folded into the turn so far once one has been, else the turn's own uuid when the host vouches it is the client event's own (delivered content such as a Slack owner ping). Absent on turns that neither had a client uuid nor folded a user message in, on Remote Control (interactive terminal) sessions, and from older producers."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Live thinking-token estimate, digested from thinking_delta.estimated_tokens during the redacted-thinking phase (where the API otherwise streams only pings). estimated_tokens is the running total for the current thinking block; estimated_tokens_delta is the increment carried by this frame. Approximate progress for spinners/pills, not the authoritative billed output_tokens.")),IW=f(()=>u({type:T("tool_use_summary"),summary:o(),preceding_tool_use_ids:C(o()),uuid:p(),session_id:o(),timestamp:o().optional().describe("@internal ISO timestamp when the summary was created on the originating process. From internal ToolUseSummaryMessage.timestamp.")})),PW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("memory_recall"),mode:B(["select","synthesize"]).describe("How memories were surfaced: 'select' returns full file bodies chosen by the parallel selector; 'synthesize' returns a Sonnet-authored paragraph distilled from many tiny memories."),memories:C(u({path:o().describe("Absolute path to the memory file, a synthesis sentinel of the form `<synthesis:DIR>` when mode is 'synthesize', or an https URL when scope is 'organization'."),scope:B(["personal","team","organization"]),content:o().optional().describe("The surfaced memory body. Always present for 'synthesize' mode and 'organization' scope (neither has an on-disk path to lazy-load from); absent for file-backed 'select' entries (renderers lazy-load from path).")})),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe('Emitted when the memory recall supervisor surfaces relevant memories into the turn. Mirrors the CLI relevant_memories attachment so SDK renderers can show "Recalled from memory" inline.')),MW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("elicitation_complete"),mcp_server_name:o(),elicitation_id:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Emitted when an MCP server confirms that a URL-mode elicitation is complete.")),xW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("permission_denied"),tool_name:o(),tool_use_id:o(),agent_id:o().optional().describe("Subagent ID when the denied tool call originated inside a subagent. Mirrors can_use_tool for host-side routing."),decision_reason_type:o().optional().describe("Discriminator from PermissionDecisionReason (e.g. 'classifier', 'asyncAgent', 'mode', 'rule')."),decision_reason_code:B(bsr).optional().describe("@internal A closed-set code for a reason a host can act on, beside decision_reason_type (whose values are unchanged): 'outside_reads_blocked' (permissions.blockReadsOutsideWorkingDirectories refused the path), 'memory_paused' (memory is paused for the session), 'classifier_transcript_too_long' (the auto-mode classifier's transcript exceeded its context window). Absent for every other reason; new values are additive."),decision_reason:o().optional().describe("Human-readable reason from the deciding component, when available."),message:o().describe("The rejection message returned to the model in the tool_result."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Emitted when a tool call is auto-denied without an interactive permission prompt (e.g. auto-mode classifier, dontAsk mode, headless-agent auto-deny, or a deny rule). With a permission prompt surface (stdio/SDK canUseTool), the 'ask' path surfaces via a can_use_tool control_request and this event covers the 'deny' short-circuit. Without one (bare -p / SDK query() with no canUseTool), 'ask' decisions are terminal, so this event also covers those implicit denials. Best-effort advisory: in rare races a denial can book without a frame or a frame can lack a booking twin \u2014 result.permission_denials is the authoritative record. Denials that resolve before canUseTool runs \u2014 PreToolUse hook denies, deny-rule overrides of hook allow/ask decisions, and file-tool calls (Read, Edit, Write) refused by a path-scoped deny rule \u2014 are not covered here, and neither is the MCP --permission-prompt-tool surface (the prompt tool is the host there).")),DW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("permission_check_status"),tool_use_id:o(),agent_id:o().optional().describe("Subagent ID when the tool call originated inside a subagent, as on permission_denied."),status:B(["checking","done"]).describe("'checking': the call has waited about four seconds on its check and is still waiting. 'done': that wait is over."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal A tool call has been waiting on its automatic permission check (the auto-mode classifier) for longer than usual. It lets a host say that the step is being checked, where it would otherwise show a silent pause that looks like a running tool. Most checks answer within about four seconds and send nothing. The wait can include the rest of the model's streaming reply, or a dialog the host is showing about the check: prefer those indicators. Every 'checking' is followed by a 'done' for the same tool_use_id. 'done' does not say whether the call was allowed: the tool's result, permission_denied or a can_use_tool request does. One call can send more than one pair. Display-only and best-effort: a frame can be lost, so also stop showing it at any other frame for this tool_use_id or at the end of the turn. Emitted on the output stream of -p/SDK sessions.")),LW=f(()=>u({type:T("prompt_suggestion"),suggestion:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Predicted next user prompt, emitted after each turn when promptSuggestions is enabled.")),K8=f(()=>u({type:T("attachment"),attachment:oe().describe("Internal Attachment discriminated union (at-mentioned files, IDE selections, pasted images, structured output, deferred tool-use). Wire shape pending a dedicated SDKAttachment schema."),timestamp:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when the engine yields an AttachmentMessage into the turn stream. Carries user-attached content (at-mentioned files, IDE selections, pasted media) and loop-attached data (structured output, deferred tool-use payloads). SDKResultMessage.structured_output and .deferred_tool_use are derived from these frames. From internal QueryEvent 'attachment'.")),H8=f(()=>u({type:T("tombstone"),message:oe().describe("The internal Message being tombstoned. Wire shape pending a dedicated schema."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when a previously-yielded message is superseded or removed from the transcript (e.g., streaming\u2192non-streaming fallback removes a partial orphan). Consumers that render or persist the stream should remove the referenced message. From internal QueryEvent 'tombstone'.")),UW=f(()=>u({type:T("conversation_reset"),new_conversation_id:p(),uuid:p(),session_id:o(),trigger:B(["clear","plan_mode_exit","fresh_session","onboarding"]).optional().describe("What discarded the conversation: 'clear' is the /clear command (or its /reset and /new aliases), 'plan_mode_exit' is leaving plan mode with the clear-context option, 'fresh_session' is a flow that starts a fresh session to implement an approved plan, 'onboarding' is an onboarding flow re-run inside an existing session. Informational: a consumer should reset on every conversation_reset frame whatever this says, and treat an absent (older emitter) or unrecognized value as an unspecified reset."),user_message_uuid:o().optional().describe("Only with trigger 'clear': the uuid of the user message whose /clear was executed (the client's own uuid for that message when it came from a Remote Control or stream-json client, otherwise the uuid the CLI assigned to the typed command). Lets a consumer match this frame to a /clear message it has already seen, wiping once whichever arrives first, instead of relying on arrival order. Absent for the other triggers, when that uuid is not a canonical UUID, and from older emitters."),timestamp:o().optional().describe('When the reset happened, as an ISO 8601 string in UTC read from the clock of the process that performed it. Meant for display, such as the time on a "conversation cleared" row, not for ordering frames. Absent from older emitters; a consumer can fall back to the time it received the frame.')}).describe("Emitted by /clear, plan-mode exit, fresh-session, and onboarding flows. The surface should mount a fresh transcript under new_conversation_id and reset any cached session title. From internal QueryEvent 'conversation_reset'.")),F8=f(()=>u({type:T("api_metrics"),event:Kr("type",[u({type:T("start"),ttft_ms:E().int(),id:o().optional(),message_id:o().optional()}),u({type:T("end"),output_tokens:E().int(),id:o().optional()}),u({type:T("content_block_start"),id:o().optional()}),u({type:T("thinking_progress"),estimated_tokens_delta:E().int(),id:o().optional()}),u({type:T("thinking_signature"),chars:E().int(),id:o().optional()})]).describe("Per-API-call OTPS/TTFT lifecycle event. Optional id correlates parallel subagent start/end; serial callers omit it."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when a subagent's API call reports TTFT or output_tokens for OTPS (output-tokens-per-second) metering. From internal QueryEvent 'api_metrics' (ApiMetricsLifecycleEvent).")),W8=f(()=>u({type:T("os_notification"),message:o(),notification_type:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when a tool (PushNotificationTool, the Computer Use wrapper) or turn-end cleanup requests a native OS notification. The surface dispatches to its platform notification channel (iTerm2/Kitty/Ghostty/bell in the terminal; native IPC for desktop/IDE). From internal QueryEvent 'os_notification'.")),Y8=f(()=>u({type:T("apply_flag_settings"),settings:me(o(),oe()).describe("Shallow-merge flag-settings patch \u2014 same shape as SDKControlApplyFlagSettingsRequest.settings."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Output-direction counterpart to SDKControlApplyFlagSettingsRequest. Emitted when slash commands that toggle flag settings request a batched write that the surface applies to its AppState. From internal QueryEvent 'apply_flag_settings'.")),VW=f(()=>u({type:T("command_lifecycle"),command_uuid:o().describe("The queued command's uuid \u2014 the client-supplied uuid on the inbound message. Commands enqueued without a uuid (e.g. the one-shot `-p \"prompt\"` string path) emit no lifecycle events. Renamed from Engine 'uuid' to avoid collision with the universal message uuid field."),state:B(["queued","started","completed","cancelled","discarded","refused"]),wakeup_source:B(["loop_wakeup","schedule_wakeup"]).optional().describe("Only on 'started', and only when the command being started is a scheduler fire. `loop_wakeup`: a ScheduleWakeup (dynamic /loop) wakeup has fired; `scheduled_for` gives the time it was set for. `schedule_wakeup`: any other scheduled-task fire, such as a CronCreate job or the notice for scheduled tasks that were missed. The values match the UserPromptSubmit hook's `source`. A fire removed from the queue without being started (for example by an interrupt with cancel_queued, or by end_session) gets only its terminal state, without this key. Advertised by the `lifecycle_wakeup_source_v1` capability on system/init."),scheduled_for:E().optional().describe("Only beside `loop_wakeup`: the time, in epoch milliseconds, that the fired wakeup was set for; when a ScheduleWakeup call armed it, this equals the `scheduledFor` in that call's result. The fire itself can come earlier or later than that time. A host that keeps an idle session alive for a wakeup should stop waiting only when this equals the time it is waiting for: while this fire sat in the queue, a turn may have armed a newer wakeup, which is still pending. A fire that never starts (its prompt could not be prepared, the input had closed, or it was removed from the queue) sends no such frame, so the host also needs a time limit of its own past this time."),ran_ahead:T(!0).optional().describe("@internal Only on a repeated 'queued' frame, written only to the VS Code panel: the plugin hooks of this `immediate` command, typed during a turn, have run and answered ahead of its turn. The command is still queued, and its own turn prints the answer."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Fate of a queued command (slash command or queued user prompt). 'queued' when the inbound message enters the command queue; 'started' when it drains into a turn; then exactly one terminal state: 'completed' \u2014 the turn that consumed it ended cleanly; 'cancelled' \u2014 removed by cancel_async_message (the host's own, or \u2014 with Remote Control enabled (remote_control) \u2014 a remote client withdrawing a prompt that a remote client queued: such a uuid was never sent by the host, so there is nothing to resend and the frame is informational), swept by an interrupt with cancel_queued:true, caught by a pending cancel just before dispatch, or consumed into a turn that was aborted (interrupt) or died on a hard failure (model_error, prompt_too_long, ...) \u2014 cancelled-over-completed is deliberate dup-over-loss for exactly-once resenders, so a fold answered earlier in a failed turn may be re-sent (a per-fold ledger refinement is tracked separately) \u2014 but resenders must not blindly resend on 'cancelled': user-requested removals arrive as the same state, so correlate against your own cancel_async_message responses AND against any interrupt receipt's `cancelled` list (interrupt_cancel_queued_v1) and resend only cancels neither source accounts for; 'discarded' \u2014 the session ended (end_session) with the command still queued, or with a peer message still held by the receive-side policy (not preceded by 'queued' then); 'refused' \u2014 declined by the session's receive-side policy before entering the queue (outright, or after being held): not preceded by 'queued', and it will not run in this session. Known gap: folds consumed by turns ending via max_turns / hook_stopped / tool_deferred / background_requested report 'completed' even though their content may only be answered on continuation/resume. A prompt sent with requeue_if_interrupted_within_ms that an interrupt puts back reports 'queued' again (after its 'started', if that was reported), then goes on as any queued prompt does. 'completed' means the consuming turn ended, not that the result frame was delivered. Ordering relative to the result frame is per-path: a command that starts a fresh turn emits 'completed' AFTER that turn's result frame (later still when the result is held back for background tasks); a command folded into an already-in-flight turn emits 'completed' BEFORE that turn's result frame. Not a strict pairing: a terminal state may arrive for a command_uuid that never emitted 'started' (control-request ACKs, duplicate deliveries); internally-enqueued commands (cron triggers, teammate shutdown prompts, deferred-turn resume) mint a fresh uuid at enqueue and emit started/terminal without 'queued'; a turn that fails by throwing can leave 'started' without a terminal state; and a peer message the queue's own admission guard drops after acceptance (rate limit, duplicate collapse, loop guard, queued-peer cap) currently leaves 'queued' without a terminal state \u2014 on process exit a wrapper should synthesize 'discarded' for uuids it has not seen reach a terminal state. The exactly-one-terminal guarantee is per worker process, not per uuid lifetime: after CCR redelivery a 'discarded' uuid legitimately re-emits queued\u2192\u2026\u2192terminal on the next worker. Emitted on the stdout stream in -p/SDK sessions; remote transports (mobile/desktop bridge) receive the equivalent signal via delivery ACKs instead. A host driving a cloud-hosted session through the headless `--cloud` client also receives 'cancelled' (and only that state) after its interrupt with cancel_queued:true, synthesized from the session's receipt: for a uuid the host sent and did not cancel itself it means the cloud session dropped that command \u2014 it will not run; do not resend it; the frames follow the interrupt's success response, are not ordered against the interrupted turn's result, and unknown uuids (another client's prompt) may appear. From internal QueryEvent 'command_lifecycle'.")),Rqo=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("code_change_published"),provider:o().describe("Forge classification derived from the URL's shape: 'github', 'github-enterprise', 'gitlab', or 'bitbucket' for pull/merge requests, 'gerrit' for a change on a googlesource.com Gerrit host, and other values for other review systems in internal builds. A naming hint, not host trust \u2014 an unrecognized host with a PR-shaped path classifies as 'github-enterprise'. Open set; treat an unknown value as a valid provider you don't recognize, never as an error."),url:o().describe("Web URL of the published change, as printed in the command output. Unverified \u2014 do not route authenticated requests to this host without your own validation."),repo:o().describe("Repository path from the URL (owner/name on GitHub; may carry more segments on GitLab). Shape-validated (forge path segments only; exactly owner/name except on GitLab) but not verified against any forge \u2014 on GitLab a leading segment can be a nested group, so never interpret it as a host."),identifier:o().describe("Provider-native change identifier \u2014 the PR/MR (or other change) number as a string."),action:B(["created","edited","merged","commented","closed","reopened","ready","draft","auto-merge-enabled","auto-merge-disabled","pushed","checked-out","started"]).optional().describe("What the session did that produced this announcement: the flag-aware gh pr verb it ran ('created', 'closed', 'auto-merge-enabled', \u2026), 'pushed' for a push to a branch that has a PR (or a push that opened or updated a Gerrit change), 'checked-out' for gh pr checkout, or 'started' for the open change on the branch the session's working directory was on when a Claude Desktop session started or resumed (sent once, at the first initialize) \u2014 always sent by current producers, absent only from older ones."),branch:o().optional().describe("The session's working branch when it produced the change. Sent for providers whose changes have no head branch of their own ('gerrit'), so a host can place the change on that checkout, and with 'created' on any provider: the branch the create was opened from (its --head / --source-branch flag, else the working branch), so a host can show the new change before its own forge lookup answers. Absent otherwise."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal A code change from this session went out for review (a pull/merge request, or another provider's change in internal builds). Fires when the harness sees the change published or links the session to a PR \u2014 on creation, and also when the session contributes to an existing one (gh pr edit/close/ready, gh pr checkout, a push to a branch that has an open PR) \u2014 so bind on every event, not just the first; re-emission for the same URL is possible and idempotent. Provenance: values are scraped from the command's captured output (the last PR-shaped URL printed) or a gh pr view lookup. Captured output is not only the forge CLI's own text \u2014 hook output or files printed by the same command can contribute \u2014 so treat the fields as a binding hint: display them, but verify against the forge with your own credential-scoped lookup before routing authenticated requests or trusting the host. Best-effort, not exhaustive: a crash before the link, gh printing no URL to a piped capture (gh pr merge), or an unrecognized forge means no event \u2014 keep your provider-API lookup as the source of truth and treat this as the trigger.")),xqo=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("vcs_state_changed"),kind:B(["commit","push","merge","rebase"]).describe("What class of mutation was observed. New kinds may be added \u2014 treat an unrecognized kind exactly like a recognized one (something changed, go look)."),cwd:o().describe("The directory the shell was in when the command finished (so an inner cd is reflected; a git -C target is not) \u2014 a hint about where to look, never proof of what moved: confirm against the repository itself."),branch:o().optional().describe("The branch a commit landed on (from the commit summary line git prints per commit) or a push updated (from the ref-update line git prints per pushed ref, e.g. `HEAD -> other`). Commit and push events carry it; a command that pushed several branches emits one push event per branch. A best-effort hint: absent whenever attribution is uncertain (a push whose output was redirected still emits, with no branch; a push that also updated a ref whose name could not be carried emits a nameless event beside the named ones), and never a required key."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal A harness-observed shell command mutated repository state (git commit/cherry-pick, push, merge, rebase \u2014 dry runs excluded). A cache-invalidation signal with a deliberately minimal payload: beyond classification it carries the branch acted on and the directory the shell finished in, both hints about where to look, so consumers still re-read state (head, PR status) from the repository instead of decoding the event. Derived from the same detection as the tool result's structured gitOperation data, so the two agree on what happened; only a push's `branch` is attributed more strictly here (from the push's own output section) than in that data. A compound command can emit more than one kind (a merge and a rebase in one command collapse to the latter), and a push of several branches emits one push event per branch; coalesce freely. Best-effort, not exhaustive: only foreground mutations run through the Bash/PowerShell tools are observed \u2014 a backgrounded command whose confirming output had not printed at capture time emits nothing. A foreground push whose ref lines were redirected or silenced still emits, on the invoking command's zero exit code alone (so a compound that swallows a failed push's code also emits; the event is a prompt to look, never a claim).")),jW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("dev_intent"),kind:B(cKr).describe("What kind of development the evidence shows. 'ios_app' from the conversation (in the main thread or a subagent): Claude ran one of Apple's tools for an iOS SDK, an iOS destination or the Simulator (xcodebuild, xcrun or swift for an iOS SDK, destination or target, simctl, devicectl), wrote iOS source into a Swift or Objective-C file (import UIKit, an import of a UIKit header, or an .iOS platform in a package manifest), or wrote or read build settings that name an iOS SDK or target iPhone or iPad; no Swift edit is needed for these. A command counts only where the shell would run it, so one that is quoted, in a here-document or in a comment does not. Weaker evidence (an iOS deployment target, any other TARGETED_DEVICE_FAMILY setting, iOS source written into another kind of file, an iOS tool named inside a quoted script) counts only beside a .swift file that Claude wrote or edited. A macOS-only app or server-side Swift package never qualifies. 'ios_app' from the project scan: the session's git repository has an Xcode project, at its root or in its ios/, iosApp/, macos/ or apple/ folder, whose build settings name an iOS SDK or target iPhone or iPad. 'android_app' from the conversation: Claude wrote or edited a .kt, .kts or .java file and something it wrote, read, or ran is Android-specific (a manifest, the Android Gradle plugin, an android.* import, adb, a Gradle variant task such as assembleDebug, the emulator), so a Kotlin server or a multiplatform module with no Android target never qualifies. 'android_app' from the project scan: the session's git repository has an AndroidManifest.xml, or the Android Gradle plugin in a build script or version catalog, at its root or in its android/ folder. More kinds will be added; ignore a kind you do not recognize."),trigger:B(nA).optional().describe("The evidence the detection fired on, for a host's own analytics. From the conversation, it is the first platform-specific evidence seen, whichever order it and a file edit the rule required arrived in; an 'ios_app' detection with no Swift edit names the first evidence that is enough alone. 'ios_app': 'uikit_import' (import UIKit or .iOS( in text Claude wrote), 'xcode_project' (iOS build settings written, or seen in a tool result such as a pbxproj or xcconfig read), 'ios_command' (an iOS SDK, an iOS destination or target, or the Simulator's tools, in a command Claude ran). 'android_app': 'android_import' (import android. in text Claude wrote), 'android_manifest' (a manifest path or body written, or seen in a tool result), 'gradle_plugin' (the Android Gradle plugin written, or seen in a tool result), 'android_command' (adb, the emulator, sdkmanager, avdmanager, react-native run-android or a Gradle variant task). The edit values 'swift_edit', 'kotlin_edit' and 'java_edit' are reserved for rules that need no other evidence; no rule sends them yet. 'project_scan', for any kind: a scan of the session's git repository. The CLI scans when the process starts and sends the result. After each conversation_reset, it sends the latest scan's result again. A conversation whose scan did not find every kind scans again, uncached, when its first message arrives, so a kind that scan missed can come after that message. A client that needs to tell project evidence from conversation evidence checks for 'project_scan'. More values will be added; ignore one you do not recognize. Absent from CLI versions that predate the field, which send only conversation evidence."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal The conversation, or the git repository it runs in, shows a known kind of development work, for hosts that key tooling on it (Claude Code Desktop opens its iOS Simulator entry point on ios_app). Sent with trigger as its only other payload. Per conversation per process, each kind is sent at most once from conversation evidence and at most once from the project scan. Conversation evidence is sent when it first completes, or at startup when a resumed conversation already has it. Project evidence comes from a CLI process in print mode (-p), which is how the Agent SDK and Claude Code Desktop start it. It is sent at startup, after each conversation_reset, and at the first message of a conversation whose earlier scan did not find every kind. Either can arrive before system/init. A rewind or compaction never retracts it and only a conversation_reset starts over. Treat each kind as a sticky fact about the conversation: a client that needs only the kind can ignore repeats, and one that needs to know what set it off reads trigger.")),qW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("turn_preempted"),reason:o().describe("Why the turn was stopped. 'rapid_followup': the user's next message arrived before the running turn showed any output. More reasons may be added; treat an unknown one the same way."),preempted_by_uuid:o().describe("The client-supplied uuid of the queued user message the turn was stopped for. It runs next, together with any messages queued behind it."),preempted_message_uuids:C(o()).describe("The client-supplied uuids of the user messages the stopped turn was running (uuid-less members omitted; may be empty)."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal The CLI itself stopped the running turn so a user's rapid follow-up message is answered at once, exactly as a priority 'now' message would have (running shell commands are backgrounded, not killed). Sent at the moment of the stop, so it precedes the stopped turn's result frame (terminal_reason aborted_streaming or aborted_tools) and its members' 'cancelled' command_lifecycle frames (it can also precede the follow-up's own 'queued' command_lifecycle frame): a host renders that turn as superseded by the follow-up rather than as interrupted, does not resend its messages, and treats only preempted_by_uuid as picked up. At most one per burst: the turn the follow-up starts is never preempted this way. Emitted on the stdout stream in -p/SDK sessions only, and only to a consumer that declared rapidFollowupPreempt on its initialize request, while the feature's rollout flag is on: without both, the turn is not stopped at all. Explicit 'now' and 'later' priorities never produce it.")),GW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("per_turn_effort_changed"),per_turn_effort_active:O().describe("Always false: a change to true is reported only by system/init."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe(`@internal The conversation stopped sending effort per turn, because the server refused its per-turn effort message or a role:"system" message. From the retried request on, an effort change rewrites the cached prefix, for every model, until a later system/init says otherwise. Sent once, when the conversation first turns per-turn effort off, before the retried request's output, on the output stream of -p/SDK sessions; sent again only if the server refuses again after the conversation's refusals are cleared (for example by /clear or a compaction). It can repeat a false the host already holds. Pairs with system/init's per_turn_effort_active.`)),BW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("file_attachments_missing"),message_uuid:o().describe("The uuid of the inbound user message whose file_attachments named the files."),sent_count:E().int().describe("How many well-formed file_attachments entries that message had."),missing:C(u({file_uuid:o().describe("The file_uuid of the message's file_attachments entry."),reason:o().describe("Why, as a short code. 'download': it could not be downloaded. 'too_large': the file is over the size a session accepts, so sending it again cannot help. 'not_found': the files route answered 404 or 410: the file is most likely deleted, or is not this session's to read, so sending it again rarely helps. 'write': it was downloaded and could not be saved. 'no_token': the session had no credential to download with. 'not_finished': the step that fetches a message's files was cut short or threw, so all its files are listed, though some may have been saved. 'count_cap': the message named more files than a session fetches, and this one was over the limit. More codes may be added; read an unknown one as 'did not arrive'.")})).describe("The files Claude was told did not arrive, each once. Never empty."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Some files a user message sent by reference (file_attachments) did not arrive where Claude can read them, and Claude was told so in that message's text or in a later note; the turn runs on without them. A host can use it to tell the person who sent them; a session with Remote Control on also sends it to its Remote Control clients itself. Sent once the message is queued, or later when a download or a save that runs beside its turn fails, or both; only for a message that reached the session through Claude Code Remote or over Remote Control, never for input a local host writes; whenever Claude is told, and for the same files. Queued counts as told: a message that a Stop then clears from the queue, or that a UserPromptSubmit hook then blocks, still sends it, though Claude never reads that message. Nothing is sent when every file arrives, when the message is refused or has no uuid (over Remote Control, one not in UUID form counts as none), for another session's message that was first held for approval, when the only files lost are those over the count limit, or for a picture that reached Claude inside the message and whose saved copy alone was lost; on claude-code-server the same holds for a text file whose copy was to be written late, at a use of the tool container. A file whose entry carries a sha256 (another session's transfer) is reported to Claude by name instead, and is listed here only under 'not_finished'. Each event states the whole list for its message: a save lost after the message was queued, or a delivery to a new worker, states it again, so keep the last one per message_uuid. If its files all arrive that time nothing is sent, and the earlier event stands. One exception: a worker that takes over a turn already begun, or is sent again a message that its transcript already holds, may fetch and save the message's files again, except pictures and files with a sha256. Its event lists only those it could not fetch or save, so it can leave out a picture an earlier event listed, and can list a file the earlier worker did save.")),KW=f(()=>u({type:T("system"),subtype:T("cloud_session_delta"),seq:E().int().positive().describe("Rises by one with each of these frames this client writes (1 for the first); never reset by an init. A reader keeps the highest it has applied and drops a lower one."),changed:C(o()).describe('The top-level keys of `cloud_session` whose value differs from the last block this client wrote (on an init or one of these frames), e.g. ["serving"] or ["calls", "connection"]; never empty. A hint for what to redraw: the block below is complete either way.'),cloud_session:BA().describe("The whole block exactly as the next init would carry it (see SDKSystemMessage.cloud_session): replace the held copy, do not merge."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Written by the headless stream-json client of a cloud-hosted session (the same client that puts `cloud_session` on its init frames), and by nothing else: the session's status changed between two inits \u2014 for instance this machine's serving word or its reason, its stream to the session (`connection`), a call it serves starting, waiting on the session's approval or ending (`calls`), a serving notice said (`notices`). Only after the first init \u2014 except from a serve-only helper (`--attach-serve`), whose stream to the session carries no init: it writes these from the start, its first (`seq` 1) listing every key of the block in `changed`, so a host that has read no init takes that frame's block as its first snapshot. At most a few per second (changes within a quarter second are written as one frame carrying the latest block); none when nothing differs. An init is always a complete snapshot, so a host that (re)attaches resynchronises from the first init it reads and applies these on top. Display-only; ignore it if you do not draw session status.")),$8=f(()=>u({type:T("set_expanded_view"),expanded_view:B(["none","tasks","teammates"]),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Hint to expand a side panel. Enum is a superset of AppState.expandedView for back-compat; do not narrow.")),To=f(()=>u({type:T("active_goal"),value:u({condition:o(),iterations:E().int(),set_at:E().int(),tokens_at_start:E().int(),last_reason:o().optional()}).nullable(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("Emitted when the user's /goal Stop hook reports met (clears) or not-yet-met (bumps iterations + last_reason). Any surface with a goal indicator re-renders from this. value is null when the goal is cleared. From internal QueryEvent 'active_goal'.")),wo=f(()=>u({type:T("autocompact_state"),value:u({enabled:O().describe("Whether auto-compact is enabled on the worker (autoCompactEnabled setting + DISABLE_AUTO_COMPACT / DISABLE_COMPACT env; DISABLE_COMPACT also disables manual /compact)."),effective_window:E().int().describe("The worker's resolved auto-compact window in tokens \u2014 resolveAutoCompactWindow output clamped to the model window, honoring /autocompact, CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_COMPACT_WINDOW, clientdata and experiment overrides."),threshold:E().int().describe("The token count where threshold-triggered compaction fires on the worker \u2014 effective_window minus the summary buffer, lowered further by CLAUDE_AUTOCOMPACT_PCT_OVERRIDE when set."),enforced:O().describe("Whether threshold-triggered compaction will actually fire at `threshold`. False when the worker defers to the API's prompt-too-long (reactive mode) or context collapse owns headroom \u2014 clients then show % of window used instead of a countdown to a compaction that won't happen."),source:B(["env","settings","clientdata","experiment","model-default","unknown-model","auto"]).describe("Which input decided the window (resolveAutoCompactWindow precedence order).")}),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe(`@internal Worker-resolved auto-compact state, emitted by CCR workers at boot, whenever the resolved state changes (/autocompact, model switch, settings change), re-checked at each turn start, and re-emitted after a conversation reset. Thin clients adopt it so the "% until auto-compact" indicator counts down to the worker's real compaction trigger instead of re-resolving against client-local state. Turn-scoped divergence is accepted: a turn running under a skill/command frontmatter model override compacts against that model's window while the frame keeps the resting model's (the local indicator shares this limitation). From sessionState.onAutocompactInputsChanged.`)),X8=f(()=>u({type:T("set_in_progress_tool_use_ids"),op:u({action:B(["add","remove"]),ids:C(o())}),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when tool execution adds/removes tool_use ids from the mid-execution set (after permission grant, before result). Surfaces use this to show which tools are running. From internal QueryEvent 'set_in_progress_tool_use_ids'.")),Q8=f(()=>u({type:T("hint_clears"),ids:C(o()),content_by_id:me(o(),o()),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when the server-side context-hint reject path reports cleared tool_use ids after a retry/fallback. The surface re-runs clearToolResultsById on its message list so subsequent turns match the API's view. From internal QueryEvent 'hint_clears'.")),J8=f(()=>u({type:T("open_message_selector"),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted by /rewind to open the message-selector overlay. Fire-and-forget \u2014 the user's selection returns through a separate channel. From internal QueryEvent 'open_message_selector'.")),Z8=f(()=>u({type:T("compact_progress"),event:Kr("type",[u({type:T("hooks_start"),hook_type:B(["pre_compact","post_compact","session_start"])}),u({type:T("compact_start"),hint_text:o().nullable().optional()}),u({type:T("compact_end")})]).describe("In-progress compaction lifecycle event."),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted while compaction is running (hook phase, compact start, compact end). Distinct from system/compact_boundary, which reports the post-compaction transcript boundary after completion. From internal QueryEvent 'compact_progress' (CompactEvent Delta-track arm).")),e4=f(()=>u({type:T("stream_mode"),mode:B(["tool-input","tool-use","requesting","responding","thinking"]),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}).describe("@internal Emitted when the engine's spinner phase changes during compaction. From internal QueryEvent 'stream_mode' (CompactEvent Delta-track arm).")),t4=f(()=>Kr("op",[u({type:T("response_length"),op:T("add"),delta:E().int(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}),u({type:T("response_length"),op:T("reset"),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})]).describe("@internal Emitted to drive the streaming-output character counter in the spinner ('add' accumulates, 'reset' zeroes on compaction-boundary swap). From internal QueryEvent 'response_length' (Delta-track).")),s4=f(()=>Kr("phase",[u({type:T("refusal_continuation"),phase:T("begin"),salvage_text:o(),uuid:p(),session_id:o()}),u({type:T("refusal_continuation"),phase:T("end"),uuid:p(),session_id:o()})]).describe("@internal Emitted when a silent refusal-continuation retry begins ('begin' with salvage_text to keep visible in the streaming preview) or ends ('end'). From internal QueryEvent 'refusal_continuation'.")),n4=f(()=>u({sessionId:o().describe("Unique session identifier (UUID)."),summary:o().describe("Display title for the session: custom title, auto-generated summary, or first prompt."),lastModified:E().int().describe("Last modified time in integer milliseconds since epoch."),fileSize:E().int().optional().describe("File size in bytes. Only populated for local JSONL storage."),customTitle:o().optional().describe("User-set session title via /rename."),firstPrompt:o().optional().describe("First meaningful user prompt in the session."),gitBranch:o().optional().describe("Git branch at the end of the session."),cwd:o().optional().describe("Working directory for the session."),tag:o().optional().describe("User-set session tag."),createdAt:E().int().optional().describe("Creation time in integer milliseconds since epoch, extracted from the first entry's timestamp.")}).describe("Session metadata returned by listSessions and getSessionInfo.")),yo=f(()=>Fe([pn(),un(),PF(),BF(),KF(),HF(),FF(),WF(),QF(),JF(),ZF(),eW(),sW(),nW(),rW(),oW(),aW(),iW(),lW(),cW(),hW(),uW(),OW(),pW(),NW(),_W(),fW(),zW(),CW(),dW(),IW(),PW(),LF(),MW(),xW(),LW(),XF(),YF(),UW(),VW(),Rqo(),xqo(),mW(),EW(),SW(),TW(),wW(),KW(),jW(),gW(),bW(),qW(),tW(),GW(),DW(),RW(),vW(),kW(),$F(),BW()]).describe("Every conversational and informational message the CLI emits on its output stream, discriminated by type (and subtype for system/result messages). Consumers should ignore types and subtypes they do not recognize: the set grows over time.")),$t=f(()=>B(["off","cooldown","on"]).describe("Fast mode state: off, in cooldown after rate limit, or actively enabled.")),Xt=f(()=>B(["free","preference","extra_usage_disabled","network_error","unknown","not_first_party","disabled_by_env","model_not_allowed","sdk_opt_in_required","pending"]).describe("Why fast mode can't serve right now. Absent when nothing blocks it (a request may still choose standard speed). A paused-after-rate-limit run is not here; it rides fast_mode_state as 'cooldown'.")),Ao=f(()=>u({text:o().describe("The label to show \u2014 already sanitized to a single line of plain text and capped at 6 columns, exactly as the terminal footer renders it after its \u25C6 glyph.")}).describe('@internal The session indicator (the CLAUDE_CODE_FOOTER_INDICATOR environment variable, else the footer_indicator_<text> capability of the main model, else bootstrap client_data.footer_indicator) that the terminal renders as a "\u25C6 <text>" pill in the prompt footer \u2014 an opaque status note operators set per cohort (e.g. to prove a test config reached the session). Carried on `system/init` and the `initialize` response so a host UI (Claude Desktop, IDE webviews) can render the same pill. Absent when nothing is configured; hosts should then render nothing. The client_data source is read from the CLI\'s cached bootstrap data, so a label configured there after that cache was last written first appears on a later `system/init`.'));var tR=f(()=>oe().describe("A JSON-RPC 2.0 message as defined by the Model Context Protocol (request, notification, or response object).")),HW=f(()=>u({matcher:o().optional(),hookCallbackIds:C(o()).describe("Opaque ids chosen by the client, one per hook function it registered for this matcher. When the hook fires the CLI sends a hook_callback control request carrying one of these ids as callback_id; the client maps it back to its function."),timeout:E().optional()}).describe("Configuration for matching and routing hook callbacks.")),inr=f(()=>u({subtype:T("initialize"),hooks:me(SA(),C(HW())).optional(),sdkMcpServers:C(o()).optional(),sdkMcpServerConfigs:me(o(),ao().omit({type:!0,name:!0})).optional().describe("Settings for the SDK-hosted MCP servers named in sdkMcpServers, keyed by server name. Sent as a separate field so a CLI that predates it ignores it; entries whose name is not in sdkMcpServers, and values that do not match this shape, are ignored rather than rejected. timeout and disableAutoBackground apply when the server is first registered; tools (the per-tool permission policy) adds its ask and deny rules on every initialize that names the server. An initialize never removes a rule; mcp_set_servers replaces them."),sdkMcpServerManifests:me(o(),u({initializeResult:me(o(),oe()).describe("The server's verbatim JSON-RPC `initialize` result object (protocolVersion, capabilities, serverInfo, instructions, ...), exactly as the in-process server produced it when the host initialized it with no client capabilities \u2014 not re-serialized or schema-parsed by the host."),toolsListResult:me(o(),oe()).optional().describe("The server's verbatim JSON-RPC `tools/list` result object. Omit when the initialize result does not advertise the tools capability, when the listing is paginated (nextCursor present), or when it could not be captured \u2014 the CLI then lists over the control channel as before.")})).optional().describe("Optional, keyed by sdk server name (each key should also appear in sdkMcpServers; other keys are ignored). Unlike sdkMcpServerConfigs \u2014 host-declared settings the CLI keeps for the server's lifetime, same shape inline on mcp_set_servers \u2014 this is a one-shot cache of the servers' own handshake output: sent on initialize only, consumed by the connect that follows it, never retained. MCP handshake results the host already obtained from its in-process servers by delivering initialize + notifications/initialized (+ tools/list) to them itself before writing this request. For each such server the CLI answers its own MCP client's initialize and first tools/list from these results and skips the notifications/initialized round trip, so registering N in-process servers costs no mcp_message control round trips before the first turn; tools/call and everything after the handshake still flow as mcp_message exactly as before. The host MUST keep answering mcp_message for every server as if this field were absent: a CLI that predates the field ignores it and performs the full per-server handshake over the control channel, and a newer CLI does the same for any server whose entry is missing or malformed, whose initializeResult.protocolVersion differs from the MCP protocol version the CLI's client requests, or that the CLI had already connected. Entries apply only to the connect that follows this initialize; they are never retained for later reconnects. Over a remote session transport the CLI uses the field only when sdkMcpServerManifestsOrigin is set (see that field); a host on the CLI's own stream-json stdio transport needs no marker. sdk_mcp_manifests_parked in the response reports what became of each entry. Absent (older hosts, the Python SDK, browser clients): unchanged behaviour."),sdkMcpServerManifestsOrigin:B(["host_per_send"]).optional().describe("The host's statement that it composed this request's sdkMcpServerManifests for this very send, from its servers' current state. Needed only by a host that reaches the CLI over a remote session transport, where an unmarked sdkMcpServerManifests is not used (a recorded registration could be handed to a later worker). The CLI also refuses a marked manifest whose session-stream frame is more than five minutes old, so capture the manifests when writing the request, not earlier; and it may still decline them when remote manifests are switched off in that CLI, or on any session where the session service has not told the CLI in the last five minutes that only the account owning the session can send events to it. sdk_mcp_manifests_parked in the response says what became of each entry. A value this CLI does not know counts as no marker. A host on the CLI's own stream-json stdio transport may omit this field; a host writing to the stdin of a remote-session worker cannot use manifests at all."),jsonSchema:me(o(),oe()).optional(),systemPrompt:C(o()).optional(),appendSystemPrompt:o().optional(),systemPromptSnapshot:O().optional().describe("Record the conversation's system prompt once and reuse it verbatim on every later request and resume. Omitted or true (the default): the prompt is rendered on the first request, systemPrompt or appendSystemPrompt included, and the record is sent as-is afterwards \u2014 even when a later launch passes different text \u2014 until compaction. false: never record; the prompt is rendered fresh every request. No effect where system-prompt recording is not yet enabled."),planModeInstructions:o().optional().describe("Custom workflow body for the plan-mode system reminder. Replaces the default code-implementation phases; the CLI still wraps it with the read-only enforcement preamble and the ExitPlanMode protocol footer."),appendSubagentSystemPrompt:o().optional().describe("@internal Additional system prompt appended to every Task-tool subagent (and propagated to nested subagents). Gated by CLAUDE_CODE_ENABLE_APPEND_SUBAGENT_PROMPT."),toolAliases:me(o(),o()).optional().describe("Map of tool-name aliases applied before name resolution. When the model emits a tool_use whose name is a key in this map, the tool execution path resolves the mapped name instead. Single-hop (no chains). See Options.toolAliases."),codeRunningMcpTools:me(o(),C(o())).optional().describe("@internal MCP tools that run code the model writes, keyed by server name as registered, each with its tool names. In auto mode no allow rule, skill, hook or plugin approves a call to one on its own: the classifier judges it, unless set_mcp_permission_mode_override pins its server to default. Declaring any also stops every PermissionRequest hook allow from changing the permission mode (its setMode update is dropped). Give names without the mcp__ prefix: a server or tool name that starts with mcp__ is skipped as malformed. Read from the first initialize only; its response lists what was declared in code_running_mcp_tools_applied. See Options.codeRunningMcpTools."),excludeDynamicSections:O().optional().describe("When true, omit per-user dynamic sections (working directory, auto-memory path) from the cached system prompt and re-inject them as the first user message. Lets cross-user prompt caching hit on a static system prompt prefix. Tradeoff: the model sees this context slightly later in the prompt, so steering on the working directory and memory location is marginally less authoritative. Has no effect when a custom (non-preset) system prompt is in use."),agents:me(o(),kA()).optional(),title:o().optional().describe("Custom session title. When provided, the session uses this title and skips automatic title generation. Has no effect on the persisted title when resuming an existing session."),skills:C(o()).optional().describe('When provided, only skills whose names match an entry are loaded into the main session system prompt, matching the exact canonical name (e.g. "my-plugin:my-skill") or a ":name" suffix of it. Display names and aliases do not match. Omit to load every discovered skill. Applies to the main session only; subagents use AgentDefinition.skills, which additionally resolves display names and aliases.'),webSearchIsolationExemptMcpServers:C(o()).optional().describe("@internal Additional MCP server names exempt from the web search / connector isolation latch. Unioned with the built-in infra-server list."),promptSuggestions:O().optional(),agentProgressSummaries:O().optional(),forwardSubagentText:O().optional(),supportedDialogKinds:C(o()).optional().describe("Dialog kinds (request_user_dialog `dialog_kind` values) this consumer's onUserDialog can actually render. The CLI treats ABSENCE as 'cannot display' and fails closed: without the kind declared here, a dialog-gated flow degrades to its no-dialog behavior (for 'refusal_fallback_prompt', the classic refusal error) instead of parking a dialog the consumer may mishandle. First-attached-client-wins on multi-client sessions; later initializes do not change it."),rendersChromeSetupOffer:O().optional().describe("@internal A client capability: this consumer renders the OfferChromeSetup tool's permission request as a Claude in Chrome setup card and answers it with updatedInput.outcome 'connected' or 'not_now'. It lets that tool join the tool list once a Claude in Chrome tool call has reported the browser extension is not connected and the session's experiment flag is on. On, never off: a later initialize may be another client's; absence leaves it as it was. Kept across worker restarts."),perTaskStopAffordance:O().optional().describe("Declares that this consumer renders a per-task stop control wired to the `stop_task` control request, so the user can stop an individual background task. When declared, an interrupt on an open-input (interactive stream-json) session spares running background agents/workflows (Stop only aborts the turn). Closed-input exception: a one-shot run (string prompt / -p closes stdin) still kills hold-back tasks at the held-result release regardless of the declaration \u2014 with stdin closed, a stop_task control could never be delivered, so the fail-closed kill stands. ABSENCE also fails closed: the interrupt kills background tasks, since the user would otherwise have no way to stop a runaway one. First-attached-client-wins on multi-client sessions; later initializes do not change it."),forwardNestedSubagentToolCalls:O().optional().describe("@internal Asks Claude Code to also send the tool_use and tool_result messages of sub-agents that other sub-agents start, and of skills a sub-agent forks, each with `parent_tool_use_id` set to the call that started it. That call is itself a tool_use in the messages of the parent sub-agent, so a consumer can place every sub-agent under the one that started it and list its tool calls. Their text and thinking stay unsent unless `forwardSubagentText` is set, which already sends all of it. ABSENCE sends none of them, as before. First-attached-client-wins on multi-client sessions; later initializes do not change it."),rapidFollowupPreempt:O().optional().describe("@internal Declares that this consumer renders the `system/turn_preempted` frame and expects Claude Code to stop a turn on its own: when a person's follow-up message arrives while the running turn has shown nothing yet, the turn is stopped as an interrupt would stop it (its messages get `command_lifecycle` 'cancelled' and its result is an aborted one) and the follow-up runs next, announced by `turn_preempted`. Honored only while the rollout flag is on. ABSENCE fails closed: the follow-up waits its turn exactly as before, because a consumer that does not know the frame would see a message it never withdrew reported cancelled. Messages arriving over the Remote Control bridge never stop a turn, whatever was declared here. First-attached-client-wins on multi-client sessions; later initializes do not change it."),plugins:C(snt()).optional().describe("Plugins to load for the session, in the same shape as the SDK `plugins` option: the stdin form of one --plugin-dir flag per entry (--plugin-dir-no-mcp when skipMcpDiscovery is set), so the launch command line does not grow with the plugin count. Loaded only by a CLI launched with --await-initialize, which reads this request during startup before any plugin work. Without that flag, on a repeated initialize, or over a remote session transport the field loads nothing; plugins_applied in the response reports whether the listed plugins are in fact loaded."),workspaceTrust:u({accepted:O().describe("Only send true after showing the user a trust dialog for `directory` \u2014 the same attestation set_cwd carries as trust_accepted."),directory:o().describe("Absolute path of the directory the user accepted, as it exists on the machine the CLI runs on. Honored only when it resolves to the trust key of the session working directory (the canonical git root when inside a repository, so a repository root and its linked worktrees agree); a network or obfuscated spelling, a path that is not a directory here, or one that resolves elsewhere records nothing. The grant covers that whole repository, exactly as the terminal trust dialog and set_cwd do, so a host whose folder sits inside a repository should name the repository root in its dialog.")}).optional().describe("@internal Host attestation that the user explicitly accepted a trust dialog for the session's working directory before launching it \u2014 the launch-time twin of set_cwd's trust_accepted/trusted_directory, for hosts whose dialog runs before the CLI is spawned and possibly on another machine than the ~/.claude.json this process reads (Claude Desktop's Code tab over SSH or WSL). Honored once, on the session's first initialize from the process that owns the CLI's stdin: the CLI records the working directory as trusted in the same store the interactive trust dialog writes (the home directory is trusted for the session only and never persisted), so the persisted-trust gates read the same answer they do in a checkout the user trusted at a terminal \u2014 project permission rules and hooks re-derive in the running process, the open-change announcement that follows this initialize already sees it, and readers evaluated at launch (project .mcp.json approval, project plugins, agent-frontmatter hooks and MCP servers) see it from the next launch on. A re-sent initialize, one arriving over a remote session transport, or one sent to a parked spare (--await-claim; send workspace_trust with claim_session instead) records nothing. workspace_trust_recorded in the response reports the outcome."),attachServeRequest:u({workId:o(),environmentId:o()}).optional().describe("@internal Claude Desktop's serve-only helper launch (`--attach-serve`) only: the tool-use work item the helper was started for and the served folder's environment id, exactly as the host polled them. Read from the opening `initialize` on the helper's own stdin and nowhere else; the helper signs its link to the session over both. Every other launch ignores it.")}).describe("Initializes the SDK session with hooks, MCP servers, and agent configuration.")),FW=f(()=>u({minTimeBeforeFeedbackMs:E(),minTimeBetweenFeedbackMs:E(),minTimeBetweenGlobalFeedbackMs:E(),minUserTurnsBeforeFeedback:E(),minUserTurnsBetweenFeedback:E(),hideThanksAfterMs:E(),onForModels:C(o()),probability:E(),lastSurveyShownTime:E().nullable()}).describe("@internal Session feedback-survey configuration for host UIs (VS Code webview, Claude Desktop) that run the survey trigger logic themselves: the same GrowthBook-driven pacing/probability values the terminal survey uses, plus the cross-surface last-shown time the host can't read. Survey responses are proxied back as tengu_feedback_survey_event log_event notifications.")),WW=f(()=>u({transcript_allowed:O().describe("Whether the box may send this sign-in's session transcript, and so may open: true for Pro and Max only. Read at session start."),every_rating:O().describe("Whether VS Code opens this box in place of its own, which follows Bad only and is sent with submit_feedback. Never true unless transcript_allowed is. VS Code keeps its own box where reports are saved on the user's computer rather than sent.")}).describe("@internal For the session survey's follow-up box that opens after every rating: an optional note, sent with the session transcript by submit_survey_feedback. Its presence also says the CLI answers that request.")),YW=f(()=>u({kind:B(["post","bundle","share","disabled"]).describe("How a submit_feedback report from this session is handled: 'post' sends it to Anthropic; 'bundle' writes it, redacted, to a zip on this machine for the user to pass on (a third-party provider or gateway, or no Anthropic credentials) and the response carries its path; 'share' is the internal-build upload; 'disabled' refuses it for a static cause (an environment variable or the nonessential-traffic setting). An org-policy refusal is not reported here: it is read late and can flip, and the per-turn system/init product_feedback_disabled carries it.")}).describe("@internal The resolved feedback mode (getFeedbackMode) for host UIs that offer a feedback dialog, so they can say up front that a report is refused or saved on this machine, instead of learning either only after the user has written and sent it.")),$W=f(()=>u({commands:C(Wt()),agents:C(lo()),output_style:o(),available_output_styles:C(o()),user_output_styles_dir:o().optional().describe("@internal Absolute path of the user-level output-styles directory this CLI lists styles from: the output-styles folder of the config home as the CLI resolves it after the settings env blocks are applied, so a CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR set in user or managed settings is honored. A host that writes a user-level style file writes it here; absent on older CLIs, where the host falls back to its own reading of CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR."),models:C(hn()),unavailable_models:C(hn()).optional().describe("@internal Models the account can see but not select (disabled: true, reason folded into description \u2014 e.g. a model the org's Zero Data Retention setting excludes). Disjoint from `models`, which stays selectable-only so consumers without disabled rendering are unaffected. Populated only for allowlisted 1P hosts that render these rows (currently the VS Code extension \u2014 UNAVAILABLE_MODELS_HOST_ENTRYPOINTS); empty for every other consumer. Omitted when empty."),account:vA(),current_model:o().optional().describe("@internal The CLI's active model at connect time. Remote Control clients (web/mobile) sync their model dropdown TO this value on connect instead of sending set_model with their own default \u2014 without it, connecting from a phone silently switches the terminal's model (CC-2659)."),current_permission_mode:de().optional().describe("@internal The CLI's active permission mode at connect time, for the same connect-time sync as current_model."),inbound_read_receipts:O().optional().describe("@internal Remote Control hosts only: true when this host reports a client message read when it leaves the command queue for a turn \u2014 the message's `processing` delivery status \u2014 and finished when the turn ends (`processed`). Absent (an older CLI, or a host that acks at receipt): a delivery status means the host received the message, not that it read it. Read per initialize; the kill switch can turn it off for a session already answered."),session_state:B(["idle","running","requires_action"]).optional().describe("@internal Session state at connect time (same values as the session_state_changed message). Lets a host that attaches to an already-running CLI tell whether a turn is in progress without having seen the transition. Absent (older CLI) \u2192 unknown."),hooks_applied:O().optional().describe("Whether the `hooks` this initialize carried were registered: true on a session's first initialize, and on a repeated initialize from the process that owns the CLI's stdin (its set replaces the one registered earlier); false when a repeated initialize's hooks were ignored (a client joining a remote session another client configured). Absent when the request carried no hooks, and on CLIs that predate the field \u2014 those ignored `hooks` on every repeated initialize."),code_running_mcp_tools_applied:me(o(),C(o())).optional().describe("@internal The MCP tools the first initialize's codeRunningMcpTools declared, keyed by server name with tool names as sent, each once. Names are not checked against the registered servers, so a misspelled one is listed but covers nothing. Malformed entries, and servers left with no tool, are omitted. Absent when that initialize carried no codeRunningMcpTools, on a repeated initialize, and on older CLIs: treat a tool as declared only when it is listed here."),plugins_applied:O().optional().describe("Whether every plugin this initialize listed is loaded: true when each one is among the plugins the process loaded at launch (from `plugins` under --await-initialize, or from --plugin-dir), so a re-sent initialize naming the launch set also reads true; false otherwise. The `plugins` field never loads anything after launch. Absent when the request listed no plugins, and on CLIs that predate the field (those never read `plugins`)."),host_worded_asks:O().optional().describe("@internal Desktop host only: true when this session reads permissionDisplay on a PreToolUse hook's ask (coreSchemas.ts HookPermissionDisplaySchema) and sends its asks to the host as can_use_tool requests. Absent otherwise. Send permissionDisplay only when this is true, and check each can_use_tool request for the words: the CLI may send an ask without them."),workspace_trust_recorded:O().optional().describe("@internal Outcome of this initialize's `workspaceTrust` attestation: true when the attested directory resolved to the session working directory's trust key and that key is now persisted as trusted (or already was); false when nothing durable was recorded \u2014 the directory was a network or obfuscated spelling, is not a directory on this machine, resolved to another key, the grant could not be written durably, or the working directory is the home directory (trusted for this session only); false too, whatever is already persisted, when the directory, the working directory or that key carries an invisible or format character (refused as set_cwd refuses such a spelling). Absent when the request carried no accepted attestation, on a repeated or remote-transport initialize, on a parked spare's initialize (--await-claim), and on CLIs that predate the field."),sdk_mcp_manifests_parked:me(o(),B(["parked","already_connected","protocol_version_mismatch","malformed","not_honoured"])).optional().describe("What became of each entry of this initialize's sdkMcpServerManifests, keyed by sdk server name, for the entries whose name is in sdkMcpServers. 'parked': kept for the connect that follows this reply, which answers the server's MCP initialize and first tools/list from it (a later failure to replay shows up as live mcp_message frames, as when no entry was sent). 'already_connected': this CLI already had a live client for the server. 'protocol_version_mismatch': initializeResult.protocolVersion is not the version this CLI's client requests. 'malformed': the entry is not of the documented shape, reported whether or not manifests were honoured (every name in sdkMcpServers gets this when the field itself is not an object); or an honoured entry is past the limits this CLI sets on a live answer. 'not_honoured': the CLI did not use the entry: manifests or remote manifests are switched off in this CLI, the session service has not told this remote-session worker in the last five minutes that only the account owning the session can send events to it, or the request reached such a worker other than over its session stream (nothing for the host to change in any of these cases); or this CLI had read as many tool lists as it allows in a minute (send it again later); or it came over the session stream without sdkMcpServerManifestsOrigin or on a frame too old to trust (the host's to fix). Absent when the request carried no sdkMcpServerManifests or named no sdkMcpServers, and on CLIs that predate the field."),permission_mode_from_default_fallback:O().optional().describe('@internal True when current_permission_mode is auto because no settings source configured a mode and the gated auto default applied. IDE hosts only; drives the one-time "auto mode is now the default" disclosure.'),auto_default_nudge:de().optional().describe("@internal The user-settings defaultMode the one-shot auto-default nudge should offer to replace with auto. IDE hosts only; absent when the user isn't eligible."),pid:E().optional().describe("@internal CLI process PID for tmux socket isolation"),claude_code_version:o().optional().describe("Claude Code version of the process that runs this session's turns (the same value its system/init carries). Absent on CLIs that predate the field, and when the responder is not that process and does not know its version."),fast_mode_state:$t().optional(),fast_mode_disabled_reason:Xt().optional(),footer_indicator:Ao().optional().describe(`@internal The terminal's configured "\u25C6 <text>" footer pill at connect time \u2014 see SDKFooterIndicator. Absent when nothing is configured.`),feedback_survey_config:FW().optional().describe("@internal Present only when the feedback-survey surface is enabled for this host (GrowthBook gate, privacy level, and org policy all allow it). Absent means the host must not show the survey."),survey_followup_box:WW().optional().describe("@internal Sent to each session a VS Code or desktop host starts itself (not a nested child session), even when feedback_survey_config is absent. Absent on CLIs without submit_survey_feedback."),feedback_mode:YW().optional().describe("@internal How this session handles a submit_feedback report (see SDKFeedbackMode), read once at init like the other host gates here; the org-policy half of 'disabled' stays on the per-turn system/init product_feedback_disabled boolean. Absent on an older CLI, and when the only refusal is the org policy \u2192 the host behaves as it does without the field."),analytics_disabled:O().optional().describe("@internal True when the CLI has analytics/telemetry disabled for this session (privacy level, DISABLE_TELEMETRY / DO_NOT_TRACK from any settings tier, third-party provider, gateway) \u2014 the same verdict the system/init message carries. IDE hosts fold it into the gate for their own direct telemetry. Absent (older CLI) \u2192 unknown."),remote_control_auto_enable:O().optional().describe("@internal Whether the CLI resolver says Remote Control should auto-enable at session start (explicit setting \u2192 policy default \u2192 GB rollout), so IDE hosts can mirror TUI behavior. Absent (older CLI) \u2192 treat as false."),remote_control_auto_on_by_default:O().optional().describe("@internal True when remote_control_auto_enable is true because of the org/GB default rather than an explicit remoteControlAtStartup setting \u2014 mirrors replBridgeAutoOnByDefault so IDE hosts can render the same disclosure notice."),remote_control_auto_connect_default:O().optional().describe('@internal The org/GB auto-connect default alone (getCcrAutoConnectDefault, under the same remote-environment guard as remote_control_auto_enable), with every explicit copy of remoteControlAtStartup left out. IDE hosts show it for their toggle only once they have seen the settings key cleared while the session runs (/config "default" clears the legacy ~/.claude.json copy in the same step); at rest they show remote_control_auto_enable. Absent (older CLI) \u2192 hosts keep their previous display behavior.'),claude_in_chrome_at_startup:O().optional().describe(`@internal Whether an IDE host that connects Claude in Chrome itself should connect it when the session starts. True when the "Enabled by default" setting (claudeInChromeDefaultEnabled) is on, this session did not connect the server itself, and the gates the terminal applies to the setting at startup pass (claudeInChromeDefaultForHost). Sent only to a host that owns the session's startup (isVscodeHostOwnedStartupSession). Absent (older CLI, other hosts) \u2192 treat as false.`),remote_control_available:O().optional().describe("@internal Whether Remote Control can be offered at all in this deployment (isRemoteControlDeploymentAvailable: not hard-disabled by managed settings, not nested in a remote environment, first-party provider). Only host-lifetime-stable conditions; transient ones (auth state, the GrowthBook rollout gate, the async org-compliance verdict) are deliberately excluded since hosts latch this from one initialize response \u2014 see the helper docstring. Lets IDE hosts hide their Remote Control affordance where it can never work. Absent (older CLI) \u2192 treat as available."),ide_rc_auto_enable_gate:O().optional().describe("@internal IDE-side rollout kill-switch for RC auto-enable (tengu_ide_rc_auto_enable), independent of remote_control_auto_enable. Carried on the init response (not experimentGates) because the host reads it at init time, before the first-prompt-triggered gate refresh. Absent (older CLI) \u2192 treat as false."),capabilities:C(o()).optional().describe("@internal Members of system/init.capabilities that are already settled at connect time, said here so a host need not wait for the first turn to read them. The same literals under the same conditions; a subset, so a member missing here may still arrive on init. Absent when there is none to say, and on older CLIs.")}).describe("Response from session initialization with available commands, models, and account info.")),zo=f(()=>u({subtype:T("interrupt"),reason:o().optional().describe("@internal Why the turn was interrupted, forwarded to the turn's AbortSignal.reason. Tool implementations branch on it to distinguish a user-driven cancel (which suppresses error output) from other aborts. Known values: `interrupt` (user Esc/Ctrl+C), `user-cancel`, `remote-cancel`, `consumer-error`, `workflow-abort`, `stalled`, `recovery-timeout`. Open set \u2014 consumers must treat unknown values as a generic abort."),cancel_queued:O().optional().describe("When true, the interrupt also cancels every uuid-stamped main-thread command still in the queue or already dequeued for the imminent turn but not yet reachable by the abort (the first-command prewait window) \u2014 the same set the response would otherwise list under `still_queued`. Each is closed with a terminal 'cancelled' lifecycle and listed on the response's `cancelled` field. `still_queued` is then empty, except that a client driving a hosted session lists there what it can no longer recall (a send already in flight to that session, or the first prompt the session was created with) and, when the session's own sweep then cancels one of those or a send it had already delivered, follows up with a command_lifecycle 'cancelled' frame for it. (The isFoldInFlight guard cancel_async_message uses does not apply here: this request also aborts the running turn, so a fold-in-flight uuid is never delivered and is swept with the rest. A fold-in-flight uuid's queued_command attachment may already appear in the aborted turn's transcript if the abort landed after the fold's attachment yield \u2014 pre-existing leave-queued semantics; it never runs as its own turn.) Uuid-less commands (task notifications) still in the queue are also dequeued but cannot be listed; a uuid-less command already in the prewait window is unreachable by either cancel leg \u2014 the first-command prewait latch covers it (this request latches exactly like a plain interrupt; see still_queued): its turn starts aborted. When false or absent, queued commands survive the interrupt and are listed under `still_queued` \u2014 the interrupt_receipt_v1 contract is unchanged. A Stop-means-stop-everything client (a remote UI's Stop button) sets this true so one round-trip halts the session; a wrapper that wants per-uuid control leaves it false and follows up with cancel_async_message. Advertised by the `interrupt_cancel_queued_v1` capability on system/init; older CLIs ignore the field and behave as if false."),send_now:O().optional().describe("@internal True when the request is a client's Send now on a message that is waiting, not its Stop: it asks that the waiting messages reach the model now, as the terminal's send-now key does. It is not a Stop: it makes no later turn start aborted, cancels nothing that is queued, and ends no background task, subscription or other thing outside the running turn that a Stop ends. What it does depends on what the CLI holds when it reads the request, and the response's send_now says which. Without message_uuid every waiting user message counts, which may be another than the one pressed. With no user message waiting at the CLI it does nothing: a message the running turn has already taken in, or was started for, is being answered. With one waiting that is not in the queue yet (received and still being admitted, or already taken for the next turn), that the running turn is taking in at that moment, or with no turn under way, it also does nothing: the message runs when its place comes. With a person's message in the queue behind a turn under way, the CLI chooses how that turn makes way: it moves what the turn waits on to the background so that the turn takes the message in (and, if that fails, stops the turn later as a priority 'now' message does, without a marker), or it aborts that turn at once as a plain interrupt does (running tools are ended, the interruption marker is written, the result is an error_during_execution); what was queued ahead of the message still runs before it. Either way a sender learns that the turn has ended from the turn's result, not from this response. The message must already be at the CLI, or the request finds nothing waiting: a sender that still holds it delivers it first, or sends it with priority 'now', or sends a plain interrupt instead. Only the boolean true counts, and with cancel_queued:true the request is a Stop. Advertised by interrupt_send_now_v1 on system/init; a CLI without it handles the request as a plain interrupt."),message_uuid:o().optional().describe("@internal Read only beside send_now:true: the uuid of the user message that Send now was pressed on, in the very form in which that message reached the CLI (a session service may deliver it in lower case): it is compared as an exact string, as cancel_async_message compares. With it only that message counts as waiting. If the running turn has already taken it in or was started for it, or the CLI does not know it or does not count it as a person's, the request does nothing and answers 'nothing_waiting', whatever else waits. Once the request acts, it acts as without the name: everything queued decides between the move to the background and the abort, and goes along with the named message. An empty string is read as no name, and so is a null, which this schema does not admit but an encoder may write; any other value that is no string names nothing."),for_user_message_uuids:C(o()).optional().describe("@internal One to eight uuids (case ignored) of user messages whose turn this Stop is for; any other value is read as absent. The current turn is the latest one dequeued for a client's message (it may be over, or not yet started), with every turn started and message taken in since. A Stop that names one of its messages is handled as a plain interrupt (if latched, see still_queued, only for pending work that it names or that answers no client's message), and so is any Stop while the CLI knows of no current turn. Other Stops do nothing and get turn: 'spared', except a few that name queued work (sparesCurrentTurn). Always include the message that opened the turn (user_message_uuid on its first frames, before any re-run): after a restart the CLI knows no other. A turn handed over by turn_handoff has no name: any Stop may stop it, so send a plain one. The uuids are only compared: never stored, logged, sent back or shown to the model. Not read beside cancel_queued:true or send_now:true, nor by older CLIs, which answer without turn."),worker_epoch:E().int().min(1).optional().describe("@internal The worker life (system/init's worker_epoch) that this interrupt is for, as a JSON number. Written only on an interrupt that a service sends by itself and that must not outlive the worker it was meant for. Never written on a person's Stop, not even as a null or a 0 for 'unset': a Stop must hold across a restart. Read by a claude-code-server tenant alone, before every other member and whatever they say. A worker of another life takes none of an interrupt's steps (it still counts as a person's presence, as every interrupt does), marks the request processed, so that no later worker is served it, and answers with a stale_worker_epoch error; take the new number from the next system/init, not from the error's text. Any other value that is present (a null, a 0, a fraction, a string such as '7') is refused the same way with an invalid_worker_epoch error, never read as absent. Both errors carry the token as error_code and list no still_queued: they are no receipt. The worker of the stated life, every other host that handles an interrupt, and an older tenant handle the request as if the member were absent, and no capability says which a worker is; a client that relays its host's Stop to a hosted session drops the member.")}).describe("Interrupts the currently running conversation turn.")),XW=f(()=>u({still_queued:C(o()).describe("Uuids of async user messages that survive this interrupt: commands still in the queue, plus any batch already dequeued for the imminent turn but not yet reachable by the abort. An interrupt \u2014 plain or cancel_queued:true \u2014 that lands during the FIRST-command prewait window (before the first turn of the session has armed a controller) is additionally LATCHED, scoped to the user-intent work pending at that instant \u2014 the batch already dequeued and parked for the imminent turn, plus the user-intent main-thread commands then in the queue (the work this list enumerates): the first turn to arm that carries any of that doomed work starts already aborted, exactly once, so the listed prewait batch is delivered into an immediately-aborted turn, its frames and result flowing through the normal abort path, instead of running to completion. A turn carrying none of it arms live and leaves the latch waiting: a system delivery turn (for example a replayed host event), or a prompt enqueued after the interrupt \u2014 post-interrupt work is never coalesced with the doomed work and never dies to the latch, so the Stop kills exactly what this receipt listed. The latch is released when the doomed work is retired without arming: if a parked prewait batch is entirely cancelled, the latch is released even when other queued commands remain (those arm and run normally); with nothing parked, it is released once none of the doomed commands remains queued \u2014 work enqueued after the interrupt neither holds it up nor is aborted by it. Survivors that ride any later turn run normally. These WILL run (subject to that latch) unless cancelled first (or unless the request set cancel_queued:true, in which case every uuid-stamped survivor this process holds is removed, emitted a terminal `cancelled` synchronously, and listed under `cancelled` instead \u2014 leaving here only what a client driving a hosted session can no longer recall: a send already in flight to that session, or the first prompt the session was created with; a send that client still holds on its own machine behind a send gate (it waits there until that session is ready to take it) has not gone out, so it is withdrawn and listed under `cancelled` like a queued one, and cancel_async_message can withdraw it too, while a plain interrupt leaves it held and lists it here). Cancellation granularity: uuids still in the queue are individually cancellable via cancel_async_message (the re-run of an interrupted turn that also answers for other client messages only if this interrupt was for it; see the response to that request); once a batch is dequeued and coalesced into one turn, cancelling a NON-representative member uuid is a no-op (its content still runs), while cancelling the batch-representative uuid drops the WHOLE coalesced batch \u2014 in both cases the cancel response reports cancelled:false because the message was no longer in the queue. Coverage caveats: only uuid-STAMPED messages appear (a message enqueued without a uuid still runs but is never listed, so [] does not mean \"nothing will run\"); only main-thread messages are listed (subagent-addressed messages are out of scope); and the list may include internally-enqueued uuids the client never sent (cron triggers, auto-resume continuations) \u2014 ignore unknown uuids rather than treating them as an error. Ordering: on a clean interrupt this receipt is written before the interrupted turn result; a turn that crashes during interrupt handling emits its error result on a direct-write path that may precede the receipt. Snapshot is taken synchronously with abort processing \u2014 probing the queue after the interrupted result instead always loses the race against the drain loop, which starts the next queued turn immediately."),put_back:C(o()).optional().describe("@internal Present only when not empty, and only from a CLI that advertises interrupt_requeue_taken_v1: those uuids under still_queued that an interrupt, this one or an earlier one, put back in the queue after a turn had taken them in (SDKUserMessage.requeue_if_interrupted_within_ms) and that are still in the queue. One already taken off the queue for its turn is listed under still_queued only. One interrupt puts back at most one. Each runs in a turn of its own, ahead of the other uuids listed unless one of those has a higher priority."),send_now:B(["stopped","delivering","nothing_waiting"]).optional().describe("@internal Present only when a CLI that advertises interrupt_send_now_v1 itself answers a send_now (a client that relays to a hosted session answers without it). 'nothing_waiting': no user message waited (with message_uuid, the named one did not), and nothing was done. 'delivering': one waits and this request stopped no turn, for one of these reasons: no turn is under way and the message runs when its place comes; the message is not in the queue yet, or the running turn is just taking it in; the CLI is moving what the turn waits on to the background, and may still stop the turn later if that fails; the turn is already being stopped; or the process is on its way out, and the message will not run in it. 'stopped': the running turn was aborted for what waits, and its result follows."),turn:B(["stopped","idle","spared"]).optional().describe("@internal Present only when the answering CLI read the request's for_user_message_uuids. 'stopped': handled; the turn under way was aborted, or the interrupt was latched for pending work. 'idle': handled; no turn was under way and it latched for nothing. 'spared': it named no message of the current turn, so nothing was done. A named message listed in still_queued still waits, whatever the value."),cancelled:C(o()).optional().describe("Present only when the request set cancel_queued:true \u2014 uuids of main-thread commands cancelled by this interrupt: every survivor that would otherwise have appeared under `still_queued`, including any uuid that was mid-fold at the interrupt instant (this request also aborts, so the fold never delivers it). Each listed uuid has been removed (queue-resident) or marked cancel-pending (the first-command prewait window, closed by the drain loop's backstop) and emits a terminal 'cancelled' lifecycle synchronously at the first such interrupt (a repeat interrupt over the same parked batch re-lists the uuid idempotently without re-emitting); none will run. Where one of them is the re-run of an interrupted turn that also answered for other client messages whose re-sent copies had been set aside for it, those messages' uuids are listed behind the commands' too: they were in no queue and never under `still_queued`, each has its terminal 'cancelled' lifecycle, and a repeat interrupt does not list them again. Same coverage caveats as `still_queued` (uuid-stamped main-thread only; internally-enqueued uuids may appear). Advertised by the `interrupt_cancel_queued_v1` capability.")}).describe("Result of an interrupt operation. Advertised by the interrupt_receipt_v1 capability on system/init; older CLIs send an empty success response with no still_queued field.")),Co=f(()=>u({subtype:T("can_use_tool"),tool_name:o(),mcp_server:Ke().optional(),input:me(o(),oe()),permission_suggestions:C(Ft()).optional(),blocked_path:o().optional(),decision_reason:o().optional().describe(`Human-readable reason the ask escalated, for the consent line of the host's dialog. For decision_reason_type "subcommandResults" (compound bash), this is the NESTED safety check's warning text \u2014 the wrapper itself has no text \u2014 preferring a check that requires manual approval (classifier_approvable false); treat it with the same display/policy care as a "safetyCheck" reason. May carry ANSI escapes; sanitize before rendering.`),decision_reason_type:B(Okt).optional().describe(`Structured discriminator for why auto-mode escalated. Lets SDK hosts make policy (e.g. auto-deny safetyCheck) without parsing decision_reason text. For compound bash commands this is "subcommandResults" even when a safetyCheck is nested inside \u2014 check classifier_approvable for that case, and see decision_reason: for this variant it carries the nested safety check's warning text.`),decision_reason_code:B([...bsr,"org_ask_ceiling"]).optional().describe("@internal A closed-set code for a reason a host can act on, beside decision_reason_type (whose values are unchanged, so type-keyed host policy decides as before): 'outside_reads_blocked', 'memory_paused', 'classifier_transcript_too_long', 'org_ask_ceiling' (the MCP tool's ceiling, toolPermissions, is 'ask', and an ask rule or the ceiling itself raised the ask: type 'rule' or 'other'). Absent for every other reason; new values are additive."),decision_reason_rule_source:o().optional().describe("@internal With decision_reason_type 'rule': the source of the rule in the decision reason (a PermissionRuleSource word). It names the first rule that matched, not the strongest, and says nothing of what that rule hides. For the host's logs: a host should not decide by it."),decision_reason_hook_event:B(["PreToolUse","tool.check"]).optional().describe("@internal With decision_reason_type 'hook': the event of the hook that asked. 'PreToolUse': the terminal's dialog names the hook 'PreToolUse:' followed by tool_name. 'tool.check': it names it 'tool.check'. A host can write the same, as plain text. Absent for any other hook. For display only: never answer or hide a prompt because of it."),decision_reason_hook_source:o().optional().describe("@internal With decision_reason_type 'hook': where the hook that asked comes from: 'plugin:<name>', 'plugin', 'skill:<name>', 'skill', or 'settings' for any other hook, such as one the SDK host registered. This CLI sends and passes on no other shape. It is absent when the CLI does not know it, when several plugins' check hooks asked together, or when the name is not 1 to 64 ASCII letters, digits, '.', '_' and '-' that start with a letter or digit. When several hooks ask on one call it is absent, or names the one whose reason decision_reason carries. The name is chosen by the plugin's or skill's author: joined words can spell a message, and it can look like a domain. Set it apart as a name, in quotation marks or in brackets as the terminal's label does. Show it in plain text, not through Markdown or an autolinker, and never as your own words. Untrusted, for display only: check and escape it, never put it in a URL, and never answer or hide a prompt because of it."),decision_reason_hook_plugin_id:o().optional().describe("@internal With decision_reason_type 'hook', for one plugin's hook: the plugin's id: its name, an at sign, then its marketplace, each a name as in decision_reason_hook_source. It can look like an e-mail address. The marketplace is a real one or a reserved word for how the plugin was loaded, such as 'synced' (delivered from claude.ai: on for the person's account, or attached to the session by its configuration), 'inline' (--plugin-dir), 'skills-dir' (.claude/skills/) or 'builtin'. Same care as decision_reason_hook_source; either may be sent without the other."),server_policy_ask_only:O().optional().describe("@internal Sent with decision_reason_type 'rule', and for an MCP tool's ask that has none. true = when the frame was built, nothing asked for a person on this call but the default prompt for a tool that no rule allows and, at most, a tool policy that the host itself pushed (permission_policy 'always_ask'): no other ask rule that the permission check applies to the call, no hook's or plugin's ask, no PermissionRequest hook, no organization ceiling, no plan-mode floor, no safety check, no ask or veto on a standing grant of the tool's own, no auto-mode classifier active for the call. false = something else did, or may have. It says nothing of WHY the host pushed that policy, which only the host knows; nothing of whose server the tool is (see mcp_server), so a host answers only for a tool that it holds a person's grant for; and nothing of what changes after the frame is sent. Absent on other reason types, on another tool's ask without a reason type, wherever a request is listed, mirrored or passed on again (pending_permission_requests), and from older versions."),classifier_approvable:O().optional().describe("Set when a safetyCheck is present anywhere in the decision reason (including nested inside subcommandResults for compound bash). false = at least one safety check requires manual approval (e.g. Windows path bypass, dangerous rm); true = all safety checks MAY be classifier-approved (e.g. sensitive-file paths). Absent when no safetyCheck is involved."),suppress_always_allow_rule:O().optional().describe(`True when the dialog must not offer the persistent "don't ask again" row for this ask: accepting it would write a whole-tool allow rule broader than the ask's own verb (PermissionAskDecision.suppressAlwaysAllowRule). Hosts rendering approve options should omit any persistent-rule affordance when set.`),default_to_no:O().optional().describe("True when the ask must not be approvable by a single stray keystroke (PermissionAskDecision.defaultToNo): a terminal-style prompt opens on its decline option and takes no digit shortcut. Hosts rendering approve options should not pre-select approve when set."),matched_ask_rule:u({source:o(),tool_name:o(),rule_content:o().optional()}).optional().describe("Set when a user-configured ask RULE (permissions.ask) forced this prompt but the ask carries the tool's own decision_reason \u2014 the ask-rule substitution keeps the richer tool-minted ask, so the rule rides here instead of decision_reason_type 'rule'. Hosts making policy on decision_reason_type (e.g. auto-deny safetyCheck) or running host-side auto-approval should treat asks carrying this field as rule-forced: the user's stated intent is a human prompt. Values are producer-authored but render-unsafe like decision_reason; sanitize before display."),title:o().optional(),display_name:o().optional(),tool_use_id:o(),agent_id:o().optional(),plugin:o().optional().describe("@internal The plugin whose `$` call (`$.ui.ask`, `$.tool.call`, `$.agent.spawn`) started the run this ask belongs to, by the name it is loaded under: who the terminal's dialog says the ask is from. The engine's word, which no plugin sets. Empty on a run resumed from an agent a plugin steered, whose name was not kept (the terminal says `from a plugin`). Absent for the model's own calls. Untrusted text; escape before display."),description:o().optional(),requires_user_interaction:O().optional().describe("True when one-tap Approve/Deny must not be offered: the tool's approval card IS the user-interaction surface (Tool.requiresUserInteraction(): the user responds on the card itself), OR the pending ask is localDisplayOnly (its consent disclosure cannot ride this wire and only the local dialog renders it), OR the auto-mode classifier blocked the call and it was handed to a person (decision_reason_type 'classifier' on a person-only ask), which no stored grant or automatic approval may answer, OR the MCP server's name spells a claude.ai connector's key and the server came from neither claude.ai nor whoever launched the session, so a grant keyed on the tool name must not answer it, OR the SDK host worded the ask (title and description are its consent copy, which a push cannot carry whole). In each case the user has to open the session to answer."),server_prompt:o().optional().describe("@internal A marker the session service puts on an ask it worded itself, naming which of its prompts this is (an open set). Nothing on this frame proves who wrote it: the marker is the service's word only on a stream served by the service, which removes the key from anything a session's own container wrote; anywhere else it is a claim. A host keeps showing the tool name and input whatever it says."),computer_folder:u({path:o(),computer_name:o()}).optional().describe("@internal Beside server_prompt, and as trustworthy as it is: a folder on one of the person's computers and that computer's name. Untrusted text; escape before display."),provenance_prompt_line:B(["one_at_a_time","all_at_once"]).optional().describe("@internal Only on the URL prompt that WebFetch raises mid-call, when the session's web-fetch proxy refuses an address. Says whether the session's line of such prompts was on for this call. 'one_at_a_time' = on: the call took a turn in the line, so no other prompt of that line was up when this one was raised. 'all_at_once' = off: the call joined no line, and its prompt was raised without waiting. For the host's logs: nothing in the CLI decides by it, and a host should not either."),tool_kind:B(["browser"]).optional().describe("@internal Structured tool category so SDK hosts can route to a specialized permission UI without name-matching tool_name. 'browser' = a Claude-in-Chrome MCP tool under any of its registered server names: the mcp__claude-in-chrome__* extension server, the mcp__Claude_in_Chrome__* Cowork desktop server, the Claude Desktop pane under mcp__Claude_Preview__* / mcp__Claude_Browser__* (both pane names are live during its rename rollout), or the mcp__remote-devices__* spellings bridged through Claude Code Remote (the bridged forms of the current names; the retiring Claude_Preview name has no bridged form). Open for future kinds.")}).describe("Requests permission to use a tool with the given input.")),Oo=f(()=>u({subtype:T("set_permission_mode"),mode:de(),ultraplan:O().optional().describe("@internal CCR ultraplan session marker."),from_settings:O().optional().describe("@internal The mode is the default of the creating person's settings, not a choice of the launch.")}).describe("Sets the permission mode for tool execution handling.")),QW=f(()=>u({mode:de().optional().describe("The permission mode now in effect. Headless CLIs echo it; other hosts may acknowledge with an empty object.")}).describe("Success payload answering set_permission_mode.")),No=f(()=>u({subtype:T("set_model"),model:o().nullable().optional().describe("Model to switch to. Omitted, null, or 'default' resets to the session default model."),system_prompt:o().min(1).optional().describe("@internal Replaces the custom system prompt (the --system-prompt / initialize systemPrompt slot). The session keeps sending the system prompt it has recorded, so the new text is first sent after the next compaction; under systemPromptSnapshot: false it is sent from the next turn on. The tool definitions already sent do not change. Applied only when the model request is accepted; must be non-empty (there is no revert-to-built-in form); re-send the current model for a prompt-only update; sending the text already in effect changes nothing. The CLAUDE_CODE_SYSTEM_PROMPT_GB_FEATURE per-turn read, where configured, still wins. Transports that do not implement it, and older builds, ack success without applying it."),only_if_model_is_current:O().optional().describe("@internal When true, the request does not change the model. `model` must name a model: if it is absent, null, empty or 'default', the request fails with error_code 'invalid_request'. `system_prompt` is required: without it the request fails the same way. If `model` names the session's model, the request succeeds, and `system_prompt` replaces the custom system prompt as that member describes. The request makes no server check of the model, runs no PreModelSwitch hook, and sends no model report to the host. For a model id that this build knows, two names are one model if they resolve to one model id: an alias resolves to the id that it stands for, every dated id of a version resolves to the undated id, and a [1m] tag is ignored. An id that a behaves_as mapping points at another model is a different model. If the session's model is another model, the request fails with error_code 'model_not_current' and changes nothing. The request also fails that way while the session's records of its model disagree. A fallback that changes the session's model can cause that state until its turn ends, or longer. A session whose model setting is 'opusplan', 'opusplan[1m]' or 'haiku' runs a different model in plan mode. For such a session the request always fails with 'model_not_current'. A value that is not a boolean fails with 'invalid_request'. `claude -p` and a claude-code-server worker implement this member. Remote Control refuses every value but false. A build without this member ignores it and applies the request as a plain set_model.")}).describe("Sets the model to use for subsequent conversation turns.")),JW=f(()=>u({model:o().optional().describe("@internal The model the session's next turn runs as of this answer, spelled as system/init's `model`: an alias becomes the id it stands for, where external_metadata.model keeps an alias as sent. A family alias can also step down to an older version. Under opusplan or haiku in plan mode it names the model the setting runs outside plan mode, not the model a plan-mode turn sends to the API. A refusal fallback in a running turn, before or after this answer, can move the next turn to another model. `claude -p` writes it on its stream-json control channel, a Remote Control server-mode child's included, and so does a claude-code-server tenant. claude -p's Remote Control bridge lane and interactive Remote Control leave it out.")}).describe("Success payload answering set_model. Hosts other than `claude -p` acknowledge with no payload.")),Io=f(()=>u({subtype:T("set_max_thinking_tokens"),max_thinking_tokens:E().int().nullable().optional(),thinking_display:B(ont).nullable().optional()}).describe("Sets the maximum number of thinking tokens for extended thinking. When max_thinking_tokens is null, thinking resets to the session default: any mid-session budget override is cleared (back to the spawn-time budget, if one was set), and thinking stays off for sessions that have it disabled. When max_thinking_tokens is omitted, the budget is left as it is, so a request that only changes thinking_display can leave the field out. thinking_display optionally sets the thinking display mode for the rest of the session: a value replaces the session display mode, null clears that override so Claude Code's default display handling applies again, and when omitted the display mode from session start (--thinking-display) is kept. 'highlights' returns one short title per stretch of thinking instead of a prose summary. The API accepts it only from Claude Code sessions that Anthropic hosts; if the API rejects it, the session sends 'omitted' (no thinking text) in its place from then on. A request for 'highlights' gets an error reply after such a rejection, on Amazon Bedrock, Google Vertex AI or another provider to which Claude Code sends no first-party-only beta features, when experimental betas are off (CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_EXPERIMENTAL_BETAS or organization policy), or on a Claude 3 model (except on Microsoft Foundry). The error names which of these it was; the display stays as it was, and max_thinking_tokens still applies when the request has it.")),Po=f(()=>u({subtype:T("rename_session"),title:o(),source:B(["remote","host"]).optional().describe("Who chose the title: 'remote' (the default) for a rename made on claude.ai and relayed to this process, 'host' for one the user made in the hosting application (an IDE), which the CLI counts as a user rename."),session_id:o().optional().describe("The session the title is for. When given and this process has since moved to another session (/clear, an in-session resume), the request is refused instead of naming the new session.")}).describe("Sets the user-facing title for the current session.")),Mo=f(()=>u({subtype:T("set_color"),color:o()}).describe('Sets the session accent color. Accepts an agent color name or "default" to reset.')),xo=f(()=>u({subtype:T("mcp_status"),liveReply:O().optional().describe("When true and the CLI writes to the session through CCR (a cloud session's worker, or a Remote Control worker), the success reply is published only to the clients attached at that moment, as a control_response marked live_only: true on the session's unsequenced event stream, and is not stored with the session's events; on a cloud session's worker its rows embed each server's configuration (URLs, headers, OAuth settings). Set it only if your client reads control_response frames from that stream; a reply sent while its stream is down is lost and the request must be sent again, and a reply over the live size limit comes back as a live error instead. Error replies other than that size-limit error, and every reply from hosts that do not send live replies (stdio hosts, and CLIs older than this field), are ordinary stored replies, so a client that sets it must accept either.")}).describe("Requests the current status of all MCP server connections.")),ZW=f(()=>u({mcpServers:C(io())}).describe("Response containing the current status of all MCP server connections.")),Do=f(()=>u({subtype:T("file_suggestions"),query:o()}).describe("Requests at-mention file autocomplete suggestions for a partial path prefix. Returns the same fuzzy-matched results the TUI shows.")),eY=f(()=>u({suggestions:C(u({path:o().describe("Relative to `cwd`, or absolute (e.g. files under the Claude config directory). A directory ends with a path separator."),score:E().optional()})),cwd:o().optional().describe("Absolute directory the relative suggestion paths are relative to: the working directory the file index was built for, which can lag the current one briefly after a directory change. Omitted by older CLIs and on transports that withhold local paths; resolve against the system/init cwd then.")}).describe("Response containing fuzzy-ranked file path suggestions (capped at the same limit as the TUI typeahead).")),Lo=f(()=>u({subtype:T("get_context_usage"),detail:B(["summary","full"]).optional().describe("'full' counts each category with the token-count API; 'summary' answers from the last response's usage and local estimates without the per-category token-count calls. Defaults to 'full'.")}).describe("Requests a breakdown of current context window usage by category.")),Uo=f(()=>u({subtype:T("get_session_cost")}).describe("Requests the formatted session cost summary 