CLI answers on its own stdio and no server stores or forwards the reply."),path:o().optional().describe("Absolute path of the skill's file on the machine the CLI runs on, which over SSH or WSL is not the host's. Present only where the CLI answers on its own stdio and no server stores or forwards the reply: absent on cloud, self-hosted runner and Remote Control connections, and for a skill with no file."),tokens:E().int()}))}).optional(),autoCompactThreshold:E().int().optional(),isAutoCompactEnabled:O(),messageBreakdown:u({toolCallTokens:E().int(),toolResultTokens:E().int(),attachmentTokens:E().int(),assistantMessageTokens:E().int(),userMessageTokens:E().int(),redirectedContextTokens:E().int(),unattributedTokens:E().int(),toolCallsByType:C(u({name:o(),callTokens:E().int(),resultTokens:E().int()})),attachmentsByType:C(u({name:o(),tokens:E().int()}))}).optional(),apiUsage:u({input_tokens:E().int(),output_tokens:E().int(),cache_creation_input_tokens:E().int(),cache_read_input_tokens:E().int()}).nullable()}).describe("Breakdown of current context window usage by category (system prompt, tools, messages, etc.).")),anr=f(()=>u({subtype:T("mcp_call"),tool:o().describe("Fully-qualified MCP tool name, e.g. mcp__server__tool_name. Plugin-hosted servers are ordinary MCP servers here \u2014 e.g. mcp__plugin_documents_docs__doc_export (server names are normalized: non-[a-zA-Z0-9_-] becomes _)."),arguments:me(o(),oe()).optional().describe(`Tool arguments. When input_files/output_files are declared, any string VALUE that exactly equals "{{in:NAME}}" or "{{out:NAME}}" (whole string, not a substring) is replaced with the worker-chosen absolute path of that named staged file before the call; a token naming no declared file fails the request with staging error_code=tool_error, and every declared output's "{{out:NAME}}" token must appear in arguments (the substituted path is the only way the tool learns where to write, so an unreferenced output fails the request before the tool runs). With no files declared \u2014 including expires_at/timeout_ms-only staged calls \u2014 passed through unchanged.`),expires_at:o().optional().describe("RFC3339 deadline, REQUIRED when output_files are declared (a stale buffered drain must not overwrite rows written since). Sending expires_at routes the call through the staging engine, same as timeout_ms \u2014 the response gains a staging result. UNDELIVERED requests buffer durably and drain after reattach; a drain past this instant is dropped with staging error_code=expired instead of executing stale. Once delivered to a live worker the request is acked immediately and never redelivered \u2014 a worker killed mid-run surfaces as a missing response (apply your own deadline), not a later drain. An unparseable value is treated as already expired (fail closed)."),timeout_ms:E().optional().describe("Tool-execution timeout (staging and collection have their own transport timeouts). Clamped to [1000, 600000]; default 120000. Sending timeout_ms routes the call through the staging engine \u2014 so it is always enforced when present and the response carries a staging result; omit it for a plain call. Staged calls are POSIX-worker-only: on a Windows worker any staged-field call fails with a typed staging refusal."),result_not_for_model:O().optional().describe("The caller hands the result to a program (a host that renders it, a script), not to a model, and bounds it itself. content is then the server's own content blocks as MCP defines them (the MCP SDK drops members it does not define), not what a model turn would be handed: no image is resized or put in the Messages API's shape, nothing is saved under tool-results, no embedded resource is flattened, and text blocks beside structuredContent stay. They are untrusted third-party data: any mimeType, images neither re-encoded nor bounded in pixels, each block's annotations and _meta unfiltered. The size check that protects a model's context is skipped too: no count_tokens request (which uploads the result), no truncation, no note in place of the content. Instead a result over 10485760 characters (content with its base64, structuredContent and _meta together) fails though the tool ran: a plain call answers the error subtype, a staged call staging.error_code tool_error with the text in detail, before its outputs are collected. A legacy toolResult still comes back as a string. Does not route the call through the staging engine. Absent or false: as before. Honoured only for a host on this process's own pipes, not over a remote transport nor on a lane that redacts what it persists. Feature-detected as `mcp_call_result_not_for_model_v1` in system/init.capabilities and in the initialize response's capabilities; where it is not said, and by a CLI without it, the member is ignored."),input_files:C(u({name:o().regex(/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}$/).describe('Handle referenced from arguments as "{{in:NAME}}". Unique within the request.'),lane_path:o().describe("Synced-file lane row to stage, e.g. /working/.cowork/originals/a.docx. A missing row fails with staging error_code=input_missing; the etag actually staged is echoed back in staging.inputs_used.")})).max(64).optional().describe("Declaring input_files or output_files makes this a STAGED call: rows are fetched from the synced-file lane into a private per-request temp dir the WORKER chooses (random, per-UID \u2014 the caller never sees or computes paths; it references files via tokens in arguments), the tool runs, and declared outputs are written back as lane rows (durable-at-ack PUT). The response then carries a `staging` result the caller switches on."),output_files:C(u({name:o().regex(/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}$/).describe('Handle referenced from arguments as "{{out:NAME}}" \u2014 the tool is told (via the substituted path) where to write; the worker collects from exactly that path. Unique within the request.'),lane_path:o().describe("Synced-file lane row to write, e.g. /working/report.cd. Unique within the request (two outputs on one row would self-conflict under CAS). Outputs over the 25 MiB lane cap fail with staging error_code=output_too_large."),if_match:o().min(1).optional().describe("Opaque lane etag the output row must still carry for the write to land (CAS). Omitted = unconditional last-writer-wins (an empty string is rejected, not treated as unconditional). A row that moved since fails that output with staging error_code=output_conflict and the requested bytes are not written. Redelivery of a completed CAS write re-runs and conflicts with its own prior write \u2014 treat output_conflict on a retry as possible-prior-success and reconcile by etag.")})).max(64).optional()}).describe("Invokes an MCP tool via the subprocess MCP client without a model turn. No permission check (control channel is trusted, same as other "+'subtypes). SDK-type MCP servers (config.type === "sdk") are rejected \u2014 '+"they are caller-provided, so the caller can invoke them directly without the subprocess round-trip. Result content passes through the same processing as model-turn MCP calls, unless result_not_for_model is set. Session expiry is not retried automatically; callers can mcp_reconnect and retry. UrlElicitationRequired (-32042) tries Elicitation hooks; if no hook "+"resolves, the call errors with the URL in the message \u2014 open it "+"out-of-band, then retry mcp_call. "+'STAGED calls (input_files/output_files declared) additionally stage lane rows in/out around the call \u2014 see the input_files describe. Staged failures come back as a success-subtype response whose staging field carries a typed error_code; subtype:error is emitted only when the call could not be attempted at all (server not connected, kill switch, dispatch failure) and means nothing ran. A target server that is not yet connected is brought up on demand: dispatch runs the deferred plugin/MCP startup resolution (the work a first model turn would have done) and waits up to 30s \u2014 shortened by expires_at when that is sooner \u2014 for the server to connect before answering "MCP server not connected", so a dispatch that races plugin startup (e.g. after an idle-wake reattach) succeeds instead of failing until a turn runs. Standard RPC semantics: a redelivered request_id supersedes the in-flight run (it is aborted and its response suppressed \u2014 exactly one response per request_id); conversion is idempotent, so re-running is safe. Cancellable via control_cancel_request.')),AY=f(()=>u({content:oe(),structuredContent:me(o(),oe()).optional(),_meta:me(o(),oe()).optional().describe("The result's own `_meta`, minus the server's keys under the CLI-reserved `com.anthropic/` prefix. Absent when nothing is left. The `_meta` of a block inside `content` is not filtered."),staging:Fe([u({ok:T(!0),outputs:C(u({lane_path:o(),etag:o().describe("Opaque lane etag returned by the durable-at-ack PUT."),bytes:E().int()})),inputs_used:C(u({lane_path:o(),etag:o()})).describe("Lane etag each staged input actually carried.")}),u({ok:T(!1),error_code:B(["expired","input_missing","output_too_large","output_conflict","timeout","tool_error"]),detail:o().describe("Plain-text detail for the requesting client. May carry user-document content (tool output, lane paths) \u2014 surface it to the user where helpful, but do not log it verbatim or feed it to analytics."),retryable:O().optional().describe("Present when the failure class is known (lane transport verdict, CAS rejection): true = safe to re-drive with the same request_id, false = deterministic. Absent = unknown; treat as non-retryable and switch on error_code.")})]).optional().describe("Present exactly when the request used any staged-call field (input_files, output_files, expires_at, timeout_ms) \u2014 such calls run through the staging engine even with no files, so expires_at is always honored. The error_code set is a stable cross-repo contract \u2014 extend it, never rename or repurpose members. A failure makes no guarantee about partial effects: earlier outputs may already have landed; retry with the same request_id is safe for outputs without if_match (unconditional PUTs re-land the same bytes), while if_match outputs surface output_conflict against their own prior write \u2014 reconcile by etag. On staged failures the tool may never have run, so content/structuredContent may be absent.")}).describe("MCP tool result \u2014 the content array, structuredContent, and _meta "+"from CallToolResult. Unless the request set result_not_for_model, content passes through the same processing as model-turn MCP calls (large results may be truncated or redirected to a file). Caller interprets. Staged calls additionally carry a `staging` result.")),ea=f(()=>u({subtype:T("rewind_files"),user_message_id:o(),dry_run:O().optional()}).describe("Rewinds file changes made since a specific user message.")),RY=f(()=>u({canRewind:O(),error:o().optional(),filesChanged:C(o()).optional(),insertions:E().int().optional(),deletions:E().int().optional(),skippedLinks:E().optional().describe(co)}).describe("Result of a rewindFiles operation.")),ta=f(()=>u({subtype:T("cancel_async_message"),message_uuid:o()}).describe("Drops a pending async user message from the command queue by uuid. No-op if already dequeued for execution. Also a no-op, on a worker that recognises held messages, for the queued re-run of an interrupted turn while that re-run also answers for other client messages, which cancelling it would close though nobody withdrew them; see the response.")),vY=f(()=>u({cancelled:O()}).describe("Result of a cancel_async_message operation. cancelled=false means the message was not in the queue (already dequeued or never enqueued), or that the request was declined: the uuid is that of the re-run of an interrupted turn which has still to end and also answers for other client messages. The re-run then goes on, and its result names the message. It is not declined once an interrupt that was for that re-run (one without for_user_message_uuids, or one that names a message that the re-run carries; not a send_now, and not the interrupt that a session service sends by itself when a computer is linked) has listed the uuid under still_queued: the re-run is then cancelled, and those other messages with it, each with a terminal cancelled lifecycle.")),Pqo=f(()=>Kr("kind",[u({kind:T("workspace_root")}),u({kind:T("diff_root")}),u({kind:T("repo"),repo:o().describe("A repository, as owner/name.")}),u({kind:T("as_written").describe("For a path copied from one of Claude's replies, which may be relative to the cwd of an earlier moment or to the root of the repository, or may lack its leading directories. Send it only to a session whose newest system/init lists the capability read_file_as_written_v1, and send the text without a line suffix (`:12`, `#L12`) or URL scheme. The session tries the text against the live cwd, the git root above that and its working directories. If no file is there, it looks below them for a file whose path ends in the text, among the paths its file state has held: those the file tools of the main conversation have read or written since the process started, or in the transcript it started from, and what a host has seeded or staged. A file that only a sub-agent touched is not among them, nor one that only a shell command touched, unless the command did nothing but print single files plainly (`cat f`, `head f`, `sed -n 1,9p f` and the like, or a lone `grep` that matches), with no pipe or redirect. It reads a file only when exactly one fits or, of several that fit a directory, exactly one is among those paths. The response's `absPath` names the file read. Show that path: the file can be a namesake of the one meant. A text that is `~` or starts with `~/`, a text that fits no file or several, a file found in none of the folders of `within`, and a look-up that could not be finished are refused. From such a session a refusal is final for the `within` it was asked with: asking again with a directory of the client's own choosing would turn \"two files fit\" into \"the first one wins\". A CLI that has `anchor` but predates this kind refuses it."),within:C(o()).describe("The folders the client will show a file from: 1 to 512 absolute, normalized paths of at most 4,096 characters, such as the `cwd` that system/init gave. Required: without it, or with one entry that is unfit, the request is refused. The look-up is the same whatever they are. The one file it settles on is read and named only if its path is one of them or lies below one, by its spelling alone (no symbolic link followed, no case folded; `/` holds nothing); else the request is refused as any other. So they can only narrow what is answered.")})]).describe("@internal How read_file finds the file a relative path names: a directory to resolve it against, or a look-up of the one file the text can mean (as_written)."));var sa=f(()=>u({subtype:T("read_file"),path:o(),max_bytes:E().optional(),encoding:B(["utf-8","base64"]).optional().describe("How to encode the bytes in `contents`. Defaults to utf-8 (lossy for binary); pass 'base64' to read images."),anchor:Pqo().optional().describe("@internal How to find the file `path` names, instead of resolving it against the live cwd, which a `cd` in the shell moves. workspace_root, diff_root and repo each name a directory the session looks up on each request; as_written names none, and has its contract in its own description. workspace_root is the root list_directory lists from. diff_root is the repository get_workspace_diff diffs, so a path taken from that diff opens the file the diff shows. It is refused in a session whose diff follows the shell's cwd between several repositories; a client that knows the repository should send `repo`. repo is the checkout the session declares for that repository or, failing that, diff_root when its origin remote ends in that owner/name. With those three, `path` must stay inside that directory as spelled (the check follows no symlink). Whatever the anchor, the read is gated as any other read_file is. A path that is `~`, starts with `~/` or leaves that directory, or an anchor the session cannot resolve or does not serve, is refused with the error `read denied: <path>`. Leave the field out when there is no anchor: null is refused. A CLI that predates this field ignores it and reads against the live cwd; the response's `anchor` tells the two apart.")}).describe("Read a file from the session filesystem for the remote sidebar viewer. Path is resolved against cwd by default and gated by the same read-permission rules as the Read tool.")),kY=f(()=>u({contents:o(),absPath:o(),truncated:O().optional(),encoding:T("base64").optional().describe("Set when the request asked for base64. Absent means utf-8 \u2014 including when an older CLI ignored the request's encoding field."),anchor:B(["workspace_root","diff_root","repo","as_written"]).optional().describe("@internal The kind of the request's `anchor`, set when the file was found by it. Absent when the request sent no anchor, or when an older CLI ignored it and read against the live cwd.")}).describe("File contents for the remote sidebar viewer.")),na=f(()=>u({subtype:T("list_directory"),path:o().describe('Directory to list, relative to the workspace root: the project root of the session, which is the directory the session was started in or, for a worker started ahead of its session, claimed for. A `cd` in the shell, entering a worktree, or a set_cwd after the first turn does not move it. "" or "." lists the root itself. "/" is the only separator and 1024 UTF-16 code units the most. Absolute, drive, UNC and ".." paths are refused as invalid_path, and so is a path with a backslash: a name that contains one cannot be listed. Some other names are refused as unsupported_name, for example one that ends in a dot or a space.')}).describe("@internal List the direct children (names and kinds) of one directory below the workspace root of a remote session on Linux (every cloud session; a self-hosted runner on Linux). One level per request, no file contents. No symlink is followed: a path through or to a symlink is refused. A directory that a Read deny rule or a Read ask rule covers is refused, and such children are left out: the request cannot ask anyone. A directory whose every child is left out answers an empty listing, as an empty directory does. A refusal is the control error `list denied: <code>`, with code one of invalid_path, unsupported_name, symlink_in_path (a symlink at the workspace root or below it), path_not_canonical (a symlink above it, or a directory of the path was moved or removed while it was read; after either of these two codes the same request can succeed when it is sent again, if the tree changed), read_rule, not_found, not_a_directory, os_permission_denied, workspace_moved (the session works in a worktree or another directory now; this can last until the worker is restarted), unexpected_error. A rule for the whole Read tool refuses every listing as read_rule. Any other error text means the request was not served at all: the unsupported-subtype error below, `not_claimed` from a worker that is started but not yet claimed for a session, or the refusal of a client in between that does not know the request. Any other session, and a remote session on macOS, on Windows or under WSL, refuses the request as an unsupported subtype, as an older CLI does (the wording differs between a headless session and the Remote Control bridge). A remote session on Linux answers the same way while the request is switched off remotely; the switch is read for each request, so that answer can change within a session. To open a listed entry, send read_file the listed path, \"/\" and the entry name (the name alone when the listed path is \"\"), with `anchor` workspace_root. A response without `anchor` comes from a CLI that predates it, which read that relative path against the live cwd (a `cd` in the shell moves it), so it may be another file; for such a CLI, send an absolute path: the `cwd` of the first system/init message of the session, then the listed path, then the entry name. When the `absPath` of the listing starts with \"/\", that `absPath`, then \"/\", then the entry name is such a path too. Every later system/init carries the live cwd, not the root. A session whose system/init carries an empty `cwd` names no root to build that path from.")),zY=f(()=>u({absPath:o().describe('The directory that was listed, as an absolute path: the workspace root, then the request path, so "" gives the root itself. A `cd` in the shell does not move it. Exception: a lane that redacts what it persists (a Remote Control bridge worker, a tenant worker) gets the request path as it was sent, which is never absolute. An older CLI that answers list_directory leaves the field out: the path is then built as the request text says, from the `cwd` of the first system/init message and the request path.'),entries:C(u({name:o(),isDirectory:O().describe("False for a symlink, whatever it points to: a symlink is a leaf of the tree and cannot be listed. False for a special entry too."),isSymlink:O().optional().describe("Present and true only for a symlink. The target is never read or returned."),isSpecial:O().optional().describe("Present and true only for an entry that is not a file, a directory or a symlink: a FIFO, a socket or a device. read_file does not open it. The kind is the one the directory records: for a name that something is mounted over, that is the kind of what lies underneath.")})),truncated:O().describe("True when entries were left out for their number. The listing is cut at the cap of 5000 entries for one response, after the sort by name, so the ones kept are the first in that order. In a directory with more than 50000 children the rest is dropped before the sort and before the Read rules hide any, so fewer than 5000 entries can come with it. False when no entry is returned, whatever the number of children: a listing of nothing but hidden children is the listing of an empty directory.")}).describe("@internal The direct children of the requested directory, sorted by name with plain string comparison (uppercase before lowercase), not by locale.")),CY=f(()=>u({oldStart:E().int(),oldLines:E().int(),newStart:E().int(),newLines:E().int(),lines:C(o())}).describe("@internal One unified-diff hunk of the get_workspace_diff response.")),ra=f(()=>u({subtype:T("get_workspace_diff")}).describe("@internal Requests the workspace git diff for the thin-client /diff dialog. The worker resolves one base ref for both stats and hunks (the merge-base with the base branch the session names, else the default branch, when HEAD is on another branch with commits beyond it, so committed and uncommitted changes show together, uncommitted rows first; otherwise working tree vs HEAD) and applies the standard caps (5s git timeout, 50 files, 1MB/file).")),OY=f(()=>u({diff:u({stats:u({filesCount:E().int(),linesAdded:E().int(),linesRemoved:E().int()}),perFileStats:C(u({path:o(),added:E().int(),removed:E().int(),isBinary:O(),isUntracked:O(),status:B(["added","modified","removed","renamed","copied"]).optional().describe("How the file changed, in the same comparison as this row's added and removed counts: added, removed, renamed, copied, or modified (anything else, mode changes included). A move git's rename detection misses, such as one to a file not yet added to git, arrives as removed plus added. Absent from Claude Code versions that predate it, and on the rare reply whose git status lines don't line up with its counts; treat an unknown or absent value as modified."),previousPath:o().optional().describe("Renamed and copied rows: the old path as plain text, for display only. Absent, with newPath, when either name holds a character the CLI's path display refuses (controls, bidi and format characters, default-ignorables, non-ASCII spaces, U+FFFD) or could be read as another path (a backslash, a drive prefix or leading slash, a '..' part, ' => ')."),newPath:o().optional().describe("The path as plain text where it differs from path: the new path of a renamed or copied row (whose path is git's rename form), or the name of a row git quoted (a name with non-ASCII or special characters); for display only, never to join to a directory. Absent otherwise, and for the names previousPath lists.")})),hunks:C(u({path:o(),hunks:C(CY())})).describe("Diff text by path. A perFileStats row can have no entry here and be in neither skippedLarge nor restricted; it keeps its stats. Such rows include: a binary or untracked file; a symlink in the base commit; a submodule; a file under a git filter or working-tree-encoding; an index entry marked skip-worktree or assume-unchanged whose file on disk is not what the entry holds; and a file whose path on disk cannot be proven to be the one git listed, which is one reached through a link or junction, or, on a volume blind to letter case or Unicode form (macOS and Windows by default), one whose name or folder is spelled otherwise on disk than in git's index."),skippedLarge:C(o()).describe("Paths whose diff text exceeded the per-file or aggregate size cap, so they have stats but no hunks."),restricted:C(o()).describe("Paths whose hunk content was withheld by the diff surface's content-provenance gate: read-permission rules (same gate as read_file) plus this surface's stricter file-identity checks (symlink and hardlinked worktree files are refused here even where read_file serves them; so is a path that git diff lists as changed but that git holds in neither the diff's base commit nor the index). Stats remain visible."),source:Fe([u({kind:T("working-tree")}),u({kind:T("branch"),baseBranch:o(),baseRef:o()})])}).nullable()}).describe("@internal Workspace git diff for the thin-client /diff dialog. diff is null when the workspace is not a git repo or is in a transient git state (merge/rebase/cherry-pick). Paths in skippedLarge carry no hunks entry at all \