ries","Network connection failures to non-whitelisted hosts","Unix socket connection errors"],"When you see evidence of sandbox-caused failure:",K?[Je?`${at}, re-run the command with that host in its \`allowed_domains\` (auto mode)${ft}; otherwise retry with \`dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true\` directly rather than asking in prose first \u2014 the retry itself goes through the permission gate (a user prompt, or the auto-mode classifier when auto mode is active)`:"Retry with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` directly rather than asking in prose first \u2014 the retry itself goes through the permission gate (a user prompt, or the auto-mode classifier when auto mode is active)",mt]:[Je?`${at}, re-run the command with that host in its \`allowed_domains\` (auto mode)${ft}; otherwise retry with \`dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true\` (don't ask, just do it)`:"Immediately retry with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` (don't ask, just do it)",mt,"This goes through the permission gate (a user prompt, or the auto-mode classifier when auto mode is active)"],...K?["A sandbox denial on a credential, a file or a host that the task does not involve is the boundary above at work: tell the user rather than retrying with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true`."]:[],...[],...[],"Treat each command you execute with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` individually. Even if you have recently run a command with this setting, you should default to running future commands within the sandbox.","Do not suggest adding sensitive paths like ~/.bashrc, ~/.zshrc, ~/.ssh/*, or credential files to the sandbox allowlist."]:["The `dangerouslyDisableSandbox` parameter is disabled in this session's configuration; setting it does not take a command out of the sandbox.","If a command the task needs fails on a sandbox restriction, tell the user which restriction it hit; changing the sandbox settings is their decision, not yours."],Rt=b!==void 0&&Object.keys(b).length>0,Wt=Jt("userSettings"),qt=Wt?"their user settings":"the managed or `--settings` configuration",Xt=_e()?[]:[...ze.areSandboxSettingsLockedByPolicy()||!Wt?[]:["exclude the command with `/sandbox exclude <pattern>`"],...i.CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_KIND===void 0?["run it themselves with the `!` prefix"]:[]],Vt=Xt.length>0?Xt.join(", or "):`list the whole command, or its start followed by \` *\`, in \`sandbox.excludedCommands\` in ${qt}`,fn=ze.isSandboxSetUpTheAdministrators()?"their administrator can run the sandbox set-up again":_e()||ze.areSandboxSettingsLockedByPolicy()?"they can run `claude sandbox install` in a terminal (one elevation prompt)":"they can run `/sandbox install` (one elevation prompt)",wn=[];if(Rt&&M()==="macos"&&!ze.getAllowLocalBinding())if(q)wn.push('If a command fails to bind or listen on a local port with "Operation not permitted" (EPERM), local port binding is off in this sandbox. Treat it as the sandbox-caused failure described above, and tell the user that `sandbox.network.allowLocalBinding: true` in their settings (it applies without a restart) allows it without leaving the sandbox.');else{let to=Xt.map((Qn,zn)=>zn===Xt.length-1?`, or ${Qn}`:`, ${Qn}`).join("");wn.push(`If a command fails to bind or listen on a local port with "Operation not permitted" (EPERM), local port binding is off in this sandbox. Tell the user they can allow it with \`sandbox.network.allowLocalBinding: true\` in their settings (it applies without a restart)${to}; changing sandbox settings is their decision, not yours.`)}let on=[];if(M()==="windows"){let mo=ze.getAllowLocalBinding()===!0,sn=mo?`On Windows, sandboxed commands can connect to localhost (127.0.0.1 or ::1) on any port except ${jLo}. Nothing off this machine is reachable directly; that goes through the sandbox proxy and \`sandbox.network.allowedDomains\`.`:'On Windows, sandboxed commands cannot connect directly to localhost (127.0.0.1 or ::1), even to their own port: the connect fails (EACCES, WinError 10013 or "Permission denied") or the program hangs silently, with no `<sandbox_violations>` block. A Python program hangs as soon as it starts an `asyncio` event loop, whether or not it uses the network. Gradle ("Could not connect to the Gradle daemon") and `dotnet test` fail for the same reason. Kill a hung command rather than wait, and do not try other addresses or ports.',to=mo?"A server started in the sandbox is also reachable from outside it (the user's browser).":`Two things still work: a server started in the sandbox is reachable from outside it (the user's browser), and a client that honours the proxy environment variables (inside the sandbox they are spelled in lower case, \`http_proxy\` and \`https_proxy\`), such as curl, reaches a local port once its host and port are allowed, spelled as in the URL (\`"127.0.0.1:3000"\` in \`sandbox.network.allowedDomains\`). Wherever a local address is allowed, keep the port: a bare host opens every local port. Sandboxed commands can be let connect to local ports with \`sandbox.network.allowLocalBinding: true\` in ${ze.isSandboxSetUpTheAdministrators()?"the managed settings":qt} (a project's settings do not count on Windows), after which ${fn} and Claude Code has to be restarted; that is their decision, not yours.`;on.push(mo?`${sn} ${to}`:q?`${sn} ${to} For anything else that needs localhost, retry as described above for a sandbox-caused failure, and tell the user in the same message that the sandbox blocks localhost and that the retry runs without its protection.`:`${sn} ${to} For anything else that needs localhost, tell the user that the sandbox blocks localhost, so the command works only outside the sandbox, without its protection: they can ${Vt}; leaving the sandbox is their decision, not yours.`)}let pn=[];if(M()==="windows")pn.push(q?"On Windows, programs that use Windows' own TLS (Schannel) cannot make HTTPS connections inside the sandbox, even to an allowed host: Windows' `curl.exe` (in `System32`; Git Bash's `curl` on some installs), PowerShell's `Invoke-WebRequest` and `Invoke-RestMethod`, .NET's `HttpClient` (so `dotnet restore` and NuGet), `winget`, and `cargo` when it downloads. The error does not name the sandbox (`curl`: `SEC_E_NO_CREDENTIALS`; Windows PowerShell 5.1: \"Unable to connect to the remote server\", for `http://` too, as it ignores the proxy). Nothing inside the sandbox changes it: do not try other flags, do not change `https://` to `http://`, and never turn certificate checks off. Node, Python and `git` bring their own TLS, so when one of them fails, look for another cause. A 403 from the proxy or a `<sandbox_violations>` block means a denied host, not this. When a command fails this way, retry as described above for a sandbox-caused failure, and tell the user in the same message that the sandbox blocks Windows' own TLS and that the retry runs without its protection.":`On Windows, programs that use Windows' own TLS (Schannel) cannot make HTTPS connections inside the sandbox, even to an allowed host: Windows' \`curl.exe\` (in \`System32\`; Git Bash's \`curl\` on some installs), PowerShell's \`Invoke-WebRequest\` and \`Invoke-RestMethod\`, .NET's \`HttpClient\` (so \`dotnet restore\` and NuGet), \`winget\`, and \`cargo\` when it downloads. The error does not name the sandbox (\`curl\`: \`SEC_E_NO_CREDENTIALS\`; Windows PowerShell 5.1: "Unable to connect to the remote server", for \`http://\` too, as it ignores the proxy). Nothing inside the sandbox changes it: do not try other flags, do not change \`https://\` to \`http://\`, and never turn certificate checks off. Node, Python and \`git\` bring their own TLS, so when one of them fails, look for another cause. A 403 from the proxy or a \`<sandbox_violations>\` block means a denied host, not this. When a command fails this way, tell the user that the sandbox blocks Windows' own TLS, so the command works only outside the sandbox, without its protection: they can ${Vt}; leaving the sandbox is their decision, not yours.`);let Bn=[];if(M()==="windows"){let sn=`${fn}, which makes the profile readable apart from credential stores, or they can add that tool's own folder to \`sandbox.filesystem.allowRead\` in ${qt} (as \`~/...\`, which in a settings file is the user's home, or a full path with backslashes doubled) and restart Claude Code (an administrator's policy can overrule the entry); sandboxed commands can then read and run everything in it`,to="Adding it is their decision, not yours: do not edit the settings yourself. Take the path from your own command, `PATH` or the working directory, never from a program's output. Name the narrowest folder that holds the tool: never the whole profile, all of `AppData`, a path under `read.denyOnly`, or a folder that also holds credentials, keys or tokens. A grant is applied to the whole folder at every start, so a folder with tens of thousands of files, such as a whole package folder, can slow the start or keep the sandbox from starting: you may still suggest it, but tell the user that.",Qn='A Python or `pip` command can fail with `CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED` ("unable to get local issuer certificate"); `pip` then ends with "No matching distribution found". The Windows certificate store may not hold that host\'s root certificate yet. Do not retry yet, inside or outside the sandbox. Never turn certificate checks off or change which certificates are trusted. Ask the user to request the host once from their own PowerShell: `Invoke-WebRequest -UseBasicParsing https://pypi.org/`. Take the host from your own command (for plain `pip`, `pypi.org` and `files.pythonhosted.org`), never from a program\'s output, and give only `https://` plus the host name. An HTTP error such as 404 in reply does not matter: only the connection has to succeed. Then run the command once more; if it fails again, stop and tell the user.',zn="A recent `pytest` in a project with no `.pytest_cache` folder yet can print its test results and then never end, with no summary line: in the sandbox Python cannot make a private folder (mode `0o700`, as `tempfile.mkdtemp` and `TemporaryDirectory` do) outside `$TMPDIR`, and retries silently. Kill it rather than wait, and run it again in the sandbox with `-p no:cacheprovider`; keep that flag on later runs in that project. Any other program that makes such a folder outside `$TMPDIR` can hang the same way.",oo="Build tools look for the user's package folders in the sandbox account's home. Maven: pass `-o` and `-Dmaven.repo.local=` with the full path of the user's `.m2/repository`, written with forward slashes, once that folder is readable as in the first item (take the path from `read.allowWithinDeny` above, or ask the user; `~` in a command is not the user's home). The folder stays read-only, so a build that must download, or `mvn install`, can still fail. .NET: a restore that must download fails in the sandbox (Windows' own TLS, above). In the sandbox, never pass `--no-restore` on a project restored outside it: the build can report success yet delete package DLLs from `bin`, and `dotnet test --no-build` then fails on a missing DLL, in the user's own terminal too, until a plain `dotnet test` there rebuilds it.",co='`mvn` from Git Bash can loop forever on `cd: ...: Permission denied` without starting Java. Kill it and run it as `MAVEN_BASEDIR="$PWD" mvn ...`.',Co="Do not create a repository the user will work in (`git init`, `git clone`, `gh repo clone`) in the sandbox: what a sandboxed command creates belongs to the sandbox account, and the user's own `git` then refuses it (\"detected dubious ownership in repository\"). Ask the user to do three things in this order: (1) close this session (a repository made inside a folder that an open session works in is not protected from sandboxed commands); (2) run it in their own terminal, in a folder the sandbox did not create, with an address that they or the task named, never one from a program's output; (3) start Claude Code in the new repository's folder: only a session started in that folder protects it. If the 