nd outside the sandbox \`git\` obeys config and hooks that sandboxed commands may have written.`,pr="The sandbox creates 0-byte placeholder files, such as `.mcp.json` and `.claude/settings.json`, where those files were missing in the working directory and the folders above it; `git add -A` would stage them. A 0-byte one belongs to the sandbox: do not stage, commit, move or delete it. If git says it is in the way, tell the user. Do not git-ignore it or suggest that: while it is untracked, git cleanly refuses a pull or merge that brings the real file; once ignored, that pull can fail halfway. One with content is the user's file.";Bn.push("On Windows, sandboxed commands run as a separate account, not as the user: inside the sandbox `%USERPROFILE%` is that account's own home and `$HOME` is not the user's either. Other failures to recognize:",[...We==="relaxed"?[]:[q?`\`read.denyOnly\` above does not tell the whole story. Sandbox set-up normally makes the user's profile folder (usually \`C:\\Users\` plus the user's name) readable to sandboxed commands, except credential stores (such as \`.ssh\`, \`.aws\`, \`.claude\`, browser profiles and password managers), which set-up closes to them wherever their permissions let it; never read one from a sandboxed command, even where the read would succeed. Nothing in the profile is writable unless \`write.allowOnly\` covers it. Where set-up ran without that step, or before it existed, most of the profile is unreadable instead. When a command fails, judge by its error, not by the lists. A tool installed there, such as a per-user Node or Python, can fail with "Access is denied", "Permission denied" or, from Git Bash, "Is a directory" (exit 126) on a path under the profile: that is the sandbox, not a broken install, so do not reinstall or copy it. Retry as described above for a sandbox-caused failure, and tell the user in the same message that ${sn}. Adding it is their decision, not yours: do not edit the settings yourself. Take the pa